Entity,iso,Question,Answer,Citation,Pincite_Text,Source_URL,Comment
African Union,AU,Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,"§ 41 “The African Union underscores that there is a distinction between the gravest forms of the use of force that constitute an armed attack, which entitle the injured State to invoke the right to individual or collective self-defense in accordance with Article 51 of the U.N. Charter and less grave forms of the use of force.  Whether a particular cyber operation constitutes a use of force or amounts to an armed attack should be determined on a case-by-case basis. That determination should be thoroughly substantiated on the basis of an assessment of the scale and effects of the particular cyber operation.”","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pgs. 6, 7, §§ 39, 41, 42 (2024)",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,
African Union,AU,Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Silent,,,,
African Union,AU,Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter,Silent,,,,
African Union,AU,Does a State’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,No,§ 43 “...this rule and the exceptions thereto do not apply to the conduct of non-State actors that is not attributable to States...the African Union affirms that the right of self-defense is triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility.”,"Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 7, § 43 (2024)",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,
African Union,AU,Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,Yes,§ 43 “... the African Union affirms that the right of self-defense is triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility.”,"Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 7, § 43 (2024)",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,
African Union,AU,Can a State  exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,"§ 43 “... the African Union affirms that the right of self-defense is triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility.” Para. 45: In this context, the African Union reiterates that, by virtue of their territorial sovereignty, all States are under an obligation to exercise due diligence as reflected in Section Ill above and to ensure that their territory is not knowingly used to violate the rights of other States through acts that constitute a threat or use of force, whether such acts are undertaken by organs of the State or nonState actors acting under the direction, control, or instruction of the State.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 7, §§ 43, 45 (2024)",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,
African Union,AU,Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Silent,,,,
African Union,AU,Must force be the only option available to successfully repel and armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
African Union,AU,Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Silent,,,,
African Union,AU,Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Ambiguous,"§ 42 “The African Union takes note of the views that assert that States have a right to exercise self-defense against imminent threats of the use of force. This is a controversial question on which there is a paucity of judicial precedent and a lack of unanimity among highly qualified publicists. The African Union is of the view that this matter requires further study and deliberation between States taking into consideration both the unique characteristics of cyberspace and cyber-operations and the implications that any rules that may emerge in relation to this question may have for the integrity of the prohibitions on the threat or use of force. In this regard, the Member States of the African Union emphasize that, from a legal perspective, the Article 51 of the U.N. Charter permits States to use force individual or collective self-defense ""if an armed attack occurs"" against a U.N. Member State.”","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 7, § 42 (2024)",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,
African Union,AU,Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Silent,,,,
African Union,AU,Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,Ambiguous,"§ 42 “...the Member States of the African Union emphasize that, from a legal perspective, the Article 51 of the U.N. Charter permits States to use force in individual or collective self-defense “if an armed attack occurs” against a U.N. Member State.”","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 7, § 42 (2024)",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,
African Union,AU,Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,Silent,,,,
African Union,AU,Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
African Union,AU,Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"§ 41  The African Union underscores that there is a distinction between the gravest forms of the use of force that constitute an armed attack, which entitle the injured State to invoke the right to individual or collective self-defense in accordance with Article 51 of the U.N. Charter, and less grave forms of the use of force. Whether a particular cyber operation constitutes a use of force or amounts to an armed attack should be determined on a case-by-case basis. That determination should be thoroughly substantiated on the basis of an assessment of the scale and effects of the particular cyber operation. Generally, the criterion of scale requires an examination of elements such as the duration of the attack, the nature of the targets attacked, the locations of the targets attacked, and the types of weapons used, while the criterion of effects measures the extent of the damage caused by the attack.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pgs. 6, 7, §§ 39, 41 (2024)",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,
African Union,AU,Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Yes,§ 41 “Whether a particular cyber operation constitutes a use of force or amounts to an armed attack should be determined on a case-by-case basis. The determination should be thoroughly substantiated on the basis of an assessment of the scale and effects of the particular cyber operation.”,"Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 7, § 41 (2024)",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,
African Union,AU,Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,"§ 39 “...a cyber operation, depending on its scale and effect, would amount to use of force if it is expected to cause physical damage, injury, or death, that is comparable to the use of force by an act covered by the prohibition.” The African Union underscores that there is a distinction between the gravest forms of the use of force that constitute an armed attack, which entitle the injured State to invoke the right to individual or collective self-defense in accordance with Article 51 of the U.N. Charter, and less grave forms of the use of force. Whether a particular cyber operation constitutes a use of force or amounts to an armed attack should be determined on a case-by-case basis. That determination should be thoroughly substantiated on the basis of an assessment of the scale and effects of the particular cyber operation. Generally, the criterion of scale requires an examination of elements such as the duration of the attack, the nature of the targets attacked, the locations of the targets attacked, and the types of weapons used, while the criterion of effects measures the extent of the damage caused by the attack.","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 6, § 39 (2024)",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,
African Union,AU,Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Yes,"§ 39 “The African Union is of the view that cyber operations would fall within the scope of the prohibition of the use of force when the scale and effects of the operation are comparable to those of a conventional act of violence covered by the prohibition. In particular, a cyber operation, depending on its scale and effect, would amount to use of force if it is expected to cause physical damage, injury, or death, that is comparable to the use of force by an act covered by the prohibition.” § 41 “Whether a particular cyber operation constitutes a use of force or amounts to an armed attack should be determined on a case-by-case basis. The determination should be thoroughly substantiated on the basis of an assessment of the scale and effects of the particular cyber operation.”","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 6, 7, § 39, 41 (2024)",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,
African Union,AU,"Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",Ambiguous,"§ 39 “...In particular, a cyber operation, depending on its scale and effect, would amount to use of force if it is expected to cause physical damage, injury, or death, that is comparable to the use of force by an act covered by the prohibition.” §40 “For example, a cyber operation that destroys, inflicts damage, or permanently disables critical infrastructure or civilian objects within a State, may be considered as amounting to a use of force under international law. Similarly, a cyber operation that targets a military asset by destroying, damaging, or deactivating a missile defense system, could constitute a violation of the prohibition on the use of force.” § 41 “The African Union underscores that there is a distinction between the gravest forms of the use of force that constitute an armed attack, which entitle the injured State to invoke the right to individual or collective self-defense in accordance with Article 51 of the U.N. Charter...”","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 6, 7, §§ 39-41 (2024)",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,
African Union,AU,Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,No,"§ 41 “The African Union underscores that there is a distinction between the gravest forms of the use of force that constitute an armed attack, which entitle the injured State to invoke the right to individual or collective self-defense in accordance with Article 51 of the U.N. Charter, and less grave forms of the use of force.”","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 7, § 41 (2024)",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,
African Union,AU,Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Yes,"§ 41 “The African Union underscores that there is a distinction between the gravest forms of the use of force that constitute an armed attack, which entitle the injured State to invoke the right to individual or collective self-defense in accordance with Article 51 of the U.N. Charter, and less grave forms of the use of force.”","Common African Position on the Application of International Law to the Use of Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace, pg. 7, § 41 (2024)",https://perma.cc/7GR9-X64J?type=standard,
