Australia,AUS,1. Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,“Australia considers that the thresholds and limitations governing the exercise of self-defence under Article 51 apply in respect of cyber operations that constitute an armed attack and in respect of acts of self-defence that are carried out by cyber means.”,"Supplement to Australia’s Position on the Application of International Law to State Conduct in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/LSJ6-GTRW,
Australia,AUS,2. Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Yes,"A use of force will be lawful when the territorial state consents, it is authorised by the Security Council under Chapter VII of the UN Charter or when it is taken pursuant to a state’s inherent right of individual or collective self-defence in response to an armed attack, as recognised in Article 51 of the Charter. . . .  Australia considers that the thresholds and limitations governing the exercise of self-defence under Article 51 apply in respect of cyber operations that constitute an armed attack and in respect of acts of self- defence that are carried out by cyber means.","Supplement to Australia’s Position on the Application of International Law to State Conduct in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/LSJ6-GTRW,
Australia,AUS,2(a) Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter,Silent,,,,
Australia,AUS,3. Does a state’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,Silent,,,,
Australia,AUS,4. Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,Silent,,,,
Australia,AUS,4(a) Can a State  exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,,,,
Australia,AUS,5. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Yes,"“Any use of force in self-defence must be necessary to repel the actual or imminent armed attack and be a proportionate response in scope, scale and duration.”","Australia Statement Website, Annex B: Australia’s position on how international law applies to State conduct in cyberspace. pg. 5, ¶ 3, (2020)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Australia/Australia%20Statement%20Annex%20B%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=MIi3Nd,
Australia,AUS,5(a) Must force be the only option available to successfully repel and armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Australia,AUS,6. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Yes,"“Any use of force in self-defence must be necessary to repel the actual or imminent armed attack and be a proportionate response in scope, scale and duration.”","Australia Statement Website, Annex B: Australia’s position on how international law applies to State conduct in cyberspace. pg. 5, ¶ 3, (2020)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Australia/Australia%20Statement%20Annex%20B%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=MIi3Nd,
Australia,AUS,7. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Yes,"“Any use of force in self-defence must be necessary to repel the actual or imminent armed attack and be a proportionate response in scope, scale and duration.” “Australia considers that the thresholds and limitations governing the exercise of self- defence under Article 51 apply in respect of cyber activities that constitute an armed attack and in respect of acts of self-defence that are carried out by cyber means. Thus, if a cyber activity – alone or in combination with a physical operation – results in, or presents an imminent threat of, damage equivalent to a traditional armed attack, then the inherent right to self-defence is engaged.” “The rapidity of cyber activities, as well as their potentially concealed and/or indiscriminate character, raises new challenges for the application of established principles. These challenges have been noted by Australia in explaining its position on imminence and the right of self-defence in the context of national security threats that have evolved as a result of technological advances.” “[A] state may act in anticipatory self-defence against an armed attack when the attacker is clearly committed to launching an armed attack, in circumstances where the victim will lose its last opportunity to effectively defend itself unless it acts.”","Australia Statement Website, Annex B: Australia’s position on how international law applies to State conduct in cyberspace. pg. 5, ¶ 3, 4,5, (2020)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Australia/Australia%20Statement%20Annex%20B%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=MIi3Nd,
Australia,AUS,8. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Silent,,,,
Australia,AUS,9. Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,Yes,"“[A] state may act in anticipatory self-defence against an armed attack when the attacker is clearly committed to launching an armed attack, in circumstances where the victim will lose its last opportunity to effectively defend itself unless it acts.”","Australia Statement Website, Annex B: Australia’s position on how international law applies to State conduct in cyberspace. pg. 5, ¶ 5, (2020)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Australia/Australia%20Statement%20Annex%20B%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=MIi3Nd,
Australia,AUS,10. Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Australia,AUS,11. Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,Silent,"“In determining whether a cyber attack, or any other cyber activity, constitutes a use of force, states should consider whether the activity's scale and effects are comparable to traditional kinetic operations that rise to the level of use of force under international law” “Australia considers that the thresholds and limitations governing the exercise of self- defence under Article 51 apply in respect of cyber activities that constitute an armed attack and in respect of acts of self-defence that are carried out by cyber means. Thus, if a cyber activity – alone or in combination with a physical operation – results in, or presents an imminent threat of, damage equivalent to a traditional armed attack, then the inherent right to self-defence is engaged.”","Australia Statement Website, Annex B: Australia’s position on how international law applies to State conduct in cyberspace. pg. 5, ¶ 1, 3 (2020)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Australia/Australia%20Statement%20Annex%20B%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=MIi3Nd,
Australia,AUS,12. Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"“...if a cyber operation – alone or in combination with a physical operation – results in, or presents an imminent threat of, damage equivalent to a traditional armed attack, then the inherent right to self-defence is engaged.”","Australia Statement Website, Annex B: Australia’s position on how international law applies to State conduct in cyberspace. pg. 5, ¶ 3, (2020)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Australia/Australia%20Statement%20Annex%20B%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=MIi3Nd,
Australia,AUS,12(a) Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Silent,"“In determining whether a cyber attack, or any other cyber activity, constitutes a use of force, states should consider whether the activity's scale and effects are comparable to traditional kinetic operations that rise to the level of use of force under international law.”",Australia’s Position on the Application of International Law to State Conduct in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/9KHK-AZBT,
Australia,AUS,12(b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,"“...if a cyber operation – alone or in combination with a physical operation – results in, or presents an imminent threat of, damage equivalent to a traditional armed attack, then the inherent right to self-defence is engaged.”",Australia’s Position on the Application of International Law to State Conduct in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/9KHK-AZBT,
Australia,AUS,12(c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Yes,"“In determining whether a cyber attack, or any other cyber activity, constitutes a use of force, states should consider whether the activity's scale and effects are comparable to traditional kinetic operations that rise to the level of use of force under international law.”",Australia’s Position on the Application of International Law to State Conduct in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/9KHK-AZBT,
Australia,AUS,"12(d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",No,"“In determining whether a cyber attack, or any other cyber activity, constitutes a use of force, states should consider whether the activity's scale and effects are comparable to traditional kinetic operations that rise to the level of use of force under international law. This involves a consideration of the intended or reasonably expected direct and indirect consequences of the cyber attack, including for example whether the cyber activity could reasonably be expected to cause serious or extensive ('scale') damage or destruction ('effects') to life, or injury or death to persons, or result in damage to the victim state's objects, critical infrastructure and/or functioning.”",Australia’s Position on the Application of International Law to State Conduct in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/9KHK-AZBT,
Australia,AUS,12(e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Australia,AUS,12(f) Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Australia,AUS,12(g) Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,Yes,"“This involves a consideration of the intended or reasonably expected direct and indirect consequences of the cyber activity, including for example whether the activity could reasonably be expected to cause serious or extensive ('scale') damage or destruction ('effects') to life, or injury or death to persons, or result in damage to the victim State's objects, critical infrastructure and/or functioning.”","Australia Statement Website, Annex B: Australia’s position on how international law applies to State conduct in cyberspace. pg. 5, ¶ 1, (2020)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Australia/Australia%20Statement%20Annex%20B%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=MIi3Nd,
Australia,AUS,"12(h) Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
Australia,AUS,13. Can cyber operations that individually fall below the threshold of an armed attack aggregate into an armed attack triggering the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Australia,AUS,14. Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation intend to cause harms that qualify the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
