Australia,AUS,16. Can cyber operations causing harms sufficient to qualify the operation as an armed attack trigger a third State’s right of self-defense if it was not the target of those operations?,Silent,,,,
Australia,AUS,17. Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Yes,“The obligation to seek peaceful settlement of disputes does not impinge upon a State's inherent right to act in individual or collective self-defence in response to an armed attack. This right applies equally in the cyber domain as it does in the physical realm.”,"Australia Statement Website, Annex B: Australia’s position on how international law applies to State conduct in cyberspace. pg. 4, ¶ 4, (2020)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Australia/Australia%20Statement%20Annex%20B%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=MIi3Nd,
Australia,AUS,17(a) Must a State exercising collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation(s) constituting an armed attack have the victim state request or consent to such actions?,Silent,,,,
Australia,AUS,"18. In its response to a cyber operation or operations qualifying as an armed attack, is the injured state limited to cyber means in response?",No,"“If a State is a victim of malicious cyber activity, which is attributable to a perpetrator State, the victim-State may be able to take countermeasures (whether in cyberspace or through another means) under certain circumstances.”","Australia Statement Website, Annex B: Australia’s position on how international law applies to State conduct in cyberspace. pg. 8, ¶ 2, (2020)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Australia/Australia%20Statement%20Annex%20B%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=MIi3Nd,
Australia,AUS,19. Must a State employing the right of self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack report its actions to the UN Security Council?,Yes,"“A use of force will be lawful when the territorial state consents, it is authorised by the Security Council under Chapter VII of the UN Charter or when it is taken pursuant to a state’s inherent right of individual or collective self-defence in response to an armed attack, as recognised in Article 51 of the Charter” “Any reliance on Article 51 must be reported directly to the UN Security Council.”","Australia Mission to the United Nations, pg. 7, para. 4 Australia Statement Website, Annex B: Australia’s position on how international law applies to State conduct in cyberspace. pg. 5, ¶ 3, (2020)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Australia/Australia%20Statement%20Annex%20B%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=MIi3Nd,
Austria,AUT,1. Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,“A cyber activity may constitute an armed attack within the meaning of Art. 51 UN Charter whenever the scale and effects of the activity are comparable to those of an armed attack using kinetic means.”,"Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law. pg. 7 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,2. Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Yes,"The inherent right of individual or collective self-defence against an imminent or ongoing  armed attack is a rule of customary international law, codified in Art. 51 UN Charter,","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law. pg. 7 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,2(a) Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter,Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,3. Does a state’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,Yes,"“Given the fact that most cyber activities are not carried out by state authorities, it is important to note that acts of non-state actors can amount to an armed attack in the sense of Art. 51 UN Charter, provided that the following two conditions are fulfilled: 1) there is a “transboundary element”, e.g. the non-state actor operates from the jurisdiction of another state; and 2) the other state is harboring or otherwise substantially supporting the operations of the non-state actor under its jurisdiction, or is unable, as a consequence of the complete absence of state authority and effective control over the respective territory, to prevent or suppress the non- state actor’s operations.”","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pgs. 7-8 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,4. Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,No,"acts of non-state actors can amount to an armed attack in the sense of Art. 51 UN Charter, provided that the following two conditions are fulfilled: 1) there is a “transboundary element”, e.g. the non-state actor operates from the jurisdiction of another state; and 2) the other state is harboring or otherwise substantially supporting the operations of the non-state actor under its jurisdiction, or is unable, as a consequence of the complete absence of state authority and effective control over the respective territory, to prevent or suppress the non- state actor’s operations.","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law. pgs. 7-8 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,4(a) Can a State  exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Yes,"It is important to note that acts of non-state actors can amount to an armed attack in the sense of Art. 51 UN Charter, provided that  the following two conditions are fulfilled: 1) there is a “transboundary element”, e.g. the non- state actor operates from the jurisdiction of another state; and 2) the other state is harbouring or otherwise substantially supporting the operations of the non-state actor under its jurisdiction, or is unable, as a consequence of the complete absence of state authority and effective control over the respective territory, to prevent or suppress the  non- state actor’s operations","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 7-8 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,5. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Yes,“The response must be necessary and proportionate in order to end the attack.”,"Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 7 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,5(a) Must force be the only option available to successfully repel and armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,6. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Yes,“The response must be necessary and proportionate in order to end the attack.”,"Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law. pg. 7 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,7. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Yes,"“The inherent right of individual or collective self-defence against an imminent or ongoing armed attack is a rule of customary international law, codified in Art. 51 UN Charter, and enables a state to respond in a necessary and proportionate manner.”","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 7 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,8. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,9. Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,Yes,"“The inherent right of individual or collective self-defence against an imminent or ongoing armed attack is a rule of customary international law, codified in Art. 51 UN Charter, and enables a state to respond in a necessary and proportionate manner.”","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 7 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,10. Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,11. Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,No/Silent?,"“A cyber operation constitutes an armed attack if, as in the kinetic context, it causes significant death or injury to persons, or substantial material damage or destruction.” Pg 6 § 5: “Moreover, a cyber activity could either be part of a wider operation using force with kinetic means, an independent use of force with physical effects, or an independent use of force without physical effects.”","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law, pg. 7 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,12. Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"“A cyber activity may constitute an armed attack within the meaning of Art. 51 UN Charter whenever the scale and effects of the activity are comparable to those of an armed attack using kinetic means.” Pg 6 § 5: “Moreover, a cyber activity could either be part of a wider operation using force with kinetic means, an independent use of force with physical effects, or an independent use of force without physical effects.”","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law. pg. 7 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,12(a) Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Yes,"“A cyber operation constitutes an armed attack if, as in the kinetic context, it causes significant death or injury to persons, or substantial material damage or destruction. This is, however, also context-dependent and subject to a case-by-case analysis.”","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law. pg. 7 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,12(b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,“A cyber activity may constitute an armed attack within the meaning of Art. 51 UN Charter whenever the scale and effects of the activity are comparable to those of an armed attack using kinetic means.”,"Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law. pg. 7 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,12(c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Yes,"“An armed attack exists when force is used on a relatively large scale, is of sufficient gravity and has a substantial effect.”","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law. pg. 7 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,"12(d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",Ambiguous,"“An armed attack exists when force is used on a relatively large scale, is of sufficient gravity and has a substantial effect.”","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law,  pg. 7 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,12(e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,No,"“Not every use of force constitutes an armed attack in the sense of Art. 51 UN Charter, both in the kinetic and the cyber context.”",Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law,https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,12(f) Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Yes,"“An armed attack exists when force is used on a relatively large scale, is of sufficient gravity and has a substantial effect.”","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law. pg. 7 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,12(g) Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,"12(h) Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,13. Can cyber operations that individually fall below the threshold of an armed attack aggregate into an armed attack triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"“While one cyber activity in isolation may not constitute an armed attack, several cyber activities may still constitute such an attack if, taken together, they are sufficiently grave to reach the threshold of an armed attack.”","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law. pg. 7 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,14. Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation intend to cause harms that qualify the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,15. Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation produce reasonably foreseeable harms qualifying the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,16. Can cyber operations causing harms sufficient to qualify the operation as an armed attack trigger a third State’s right of self-defense if it was not the target of those operations?,Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,17. Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Yes,"“The inherent right of individual or collective self-defence against an imminent or ongoing armed attack is a rule of customary international law, codified in Art. 51 UN Charter, and enables a state to respond in a necessary and proportionate manner.”","Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law. pg. 7 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,17(a) Must a State exercising collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation(s) constituting an armed attack have the victim state request or consent to such actions?,Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,"18. In its response to a cyber operation or operations qualifying as an armed attack, is the injured state limited to cyber means in response?",No,“In response to a cyber activity constituting an armed attack a state may exercise its inherent right of self-defence. The response to such a cyber activity is not limited to cyber means.”,"Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law. pg. 7 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,19. Must a State employing the right of self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack report its actions to the UN Security Council?,Yes,“Any measure – also through cyber means – taken by the responding state must be immediately reported to the UN Security Council.”,"Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law. pg. 7 § 6, (2024)",https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Belgium,BEL,1. Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,"Should a cyber operation constitute an unlawful use of force, the victim State may also refer the matter to the United Nations Security Council if it considers that the situation constitutes a threat to the peace, a breach of the peace or an armed attack. In the event of a cyber operation constituting armed attack, it may invoke its right to self-defence (individual or collective) under Article 51 of the UN Charter. ... The conditions for invoking the right of self-defence (existence of an armed attack, procedural conditions, temporal conditions, necessity and proportionality criteria) are fully applicable to the cyber domain.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,2. Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,2(a) Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,3. Does a state’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another state(s)?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,4. Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,4(a) Can a State  exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,5. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Yes,"The conditions for invoking the right of self-defence (existence of an armed attack, procedural conditions, temporal conditions, necessity and proportionality criteria) are fully applicable to the cyber domain.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,5(a) Must force be the only option available to successfully repel an armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,6. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Yes,"The conditions for invoking the right of self-defence (existence of an armed attack, procedural conditions, temporal conditions, necessity and proportionality criteria) are fully applicable to the cyber domain.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,7. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Ambiguous,"The conditions for invoking the right of self-defence (existence of an armed attack, procedural conditions, temporal conditions, necessity and proportionality criteria) are fully applicable to the cyber domain.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,8. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Ambiguous,"The conditions for invoking the right of self-defence (existence of an armed attack, procedural conditions, temporal conditions, necessity and proportionality criteria) are fully applicable to the cyber domain.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,9. Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,10. Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,11. Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,No,"For an unlawful use of force between States to be classified as an armed attack, a higher threshold will be required for the right of self-defence under Article 51 of the UN Charter. The attack must, at the very least, result in substantial loss of life or extensive destruction of infrastructure or systems (at least when the destruction affects vital or military infrastructure) in order to qualify as such and give rise to the right to individual or collective self-defence.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,12. Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"In the event of a cyber operation constituting armed attack, it may invoke its right to self-defence (individual or collective) under Article 51 of the UN Charter. ... The conditions for invoking the right of self-defence (existence of an armed attack, procedural conditions, temporal conditions, necessity and proportionality criteria) are fully applicable to the cyber domain.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,12(a) Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,12(b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under Article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,"For an unlawful use of force between States to be classified as an armed attack, a higher threshold will be required for the right of self-defence under Article 51 of the UN Charter. The attack must, at the very least, result in substantial loss of life or extensive destruction of infrastructure or systems (at least when the destruction affects vital or military infrastructure) in order to qualify as such and give rise to the right to individual or collective self-defence.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,12(c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Yes,"Consequently, if the scale and effects of a cyber operation are similar to those resulting from the use of other weapons or military means (i.e. kinetic means), the concerned cyber operation can be considered as a prohibited threat or use of force.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,"12(d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",Yes,"For an unlawful use of force between States to be classified as an armed attack, a higher threshold will be required for the right of self-defence under Article 51 of the UN Charter. The attack must, at the very least, result in substantial loss of life or extensive destruction of infrastructure or systems (at least when the destruction affects vital or military infrastructure) in order to qualify as such and give rise to the right to individual or collective self-defence.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,12(e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,No,"In the event of a cyber operation constituting armed attack, it may invoke its right to self-defence (individual or collective) under Article 51 of the UN Charter.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,12(f) Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Ambiguous,"For an unlawful use of force between States to be classified as an armed attack, a higher threshold will be required for the right of self-defence under Article 51 of the UN Charter. The attack must, at the very least, result in substantial loss of life or extensive destruction of infrastructure or systems (at least when the destruction affects vital or military infrastructure) in order to qualify as such and give rise to the right to individual or collective self-defence.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,12(g) Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,No,"For an unlawful use of force between States to be classified as an armed attack, a higher threshold will be required for the right of self-defence under Article 51 of the UN Charter. The attack must, at the very least, result in substantial loss of life or extensive destruction of infrastructure or systems (at least when the destruction affects vital or military infrastructure) in order to qualify as such and give rise to the right to individual or collective self-defence.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,"12(h) Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,13. Can cyber operations that individually fall below the threshold of an armed attack aggregate into an armed attack triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"Under the theory of cumulative events, however, several events which, taken in isolation, could not be considered as armed attack, could, when taken cumulatively (with due regard to links in time, source and cause), reach the threshold of seriousness required to give rise to the right to self-defence.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,14. Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation intend to cause harms that qualify the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,15. Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation produce reasonably foreseeable harms qualifying the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,16. Can cyber operations causing harms sufficient to qualify the operation as an armed attack trigger a third State’s right of self-defense if it was not the target of those operations?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,17. Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Yes,"Under Article 5 of the North Atlantic Treaty, as confirmed in the Allied Joint Doctrine for Cyberspace Operations and as stated in the Wales Summit Declaration, “Cyber attacks can reach a threshold that threatens national and Euro-Atlantic prosperity, security, and stability. Their impact could be as harmful to modern societies as a conventional attack. We affirm therefore that cyber defence is part of NATO's core task of collective defence. A decision as to when a cyber attack would lead to the invocation of Article 5 would be taken by the North Atlantic Council on a case-by-case basis.”","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,17(a) Must a State exercising collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation(s) constituting an armed attack have the victim state request or consent to such actions?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,"18. In its response to a cyber operation or operations qualifying as an armed attack, is the injured state limited to cyber means in response?",Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,19. Must a State employing the right of self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack report its actions to the UN Security Council?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,1. Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,"“In analyzing and comparing the two laws, it can be inferred that: a CYBER  OPERATION constitutes use of force and generates the right to legitimate defense, based on Art. 51  of the United Nations Charter. If in cyberspace it has been determined that there may be cases of ""use of force,"" in  addressing those cases, “self-defense” will also be accommodated.”","OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response by Bolivia. pg. 2, question 4, (2019)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Bolivia/Response%20by%20Bolivia.%20EN.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=4V9maU,
Bolivia,BOL,2. Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,2(a) Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,3. Does a state’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,4. Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,4(a) Can a State  exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,5. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Yes,"“In any case, self-defense will always be limited by the principles of necessity, proportionality, and distinction, according to the rules of IHL.”","OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response by Bolivia. pg. 2-3, Question 4, (2019)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Bolivia/Response%20by%20Bolivia.%20EN.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=4V9maU,
Bolivia,BOL,5(a) Must force be the only option available to successfully repel and armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,6. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Yes,"“In any case, self-defense will always be limited by the principles of necessity, proportionality, and distinction, according to the rules of IHL.”","OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response by Bolivia. pg. 2-3, Question 4, (2019)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Bolivia/Response%20by%20Bolivia.%20EN.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=4V9maU,
Bolivia,BOL,7. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Yes,"“...in cyberspace as well, as in the case of nuclear weapons, “preemptive self-defense"" (imminence) can be approved.”","OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response by Bolivia. pg. 2, Question  4, (2019)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Bolivia/Response%20by%20Bolivia.%20EN.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=4V9maU,
Bolivia,BOL,8. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Yes,"“Regarding “self-defense,” there would also be the principle of immediacy. The “immediacy” requirement (as opposed to the imminence requirement) distinguishes an act of self-defense from mere retaliation. If “imminence” raises discussions, more so does “immediacy,” given how much time can pass before the effects are discovered and the culprits identified.”","OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response by Bolivia. pg. 2, Question 4, (2019)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Bolivia/Response%20by%20Bolivia.%20EN.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=4V9maU,
Bolivia,BOL,9. Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,Yes,"“...in cyberspace as well, as in the case of nuclear weapons, “preemptive self-defense"" (imminence) can be approved.”","OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response by Bolivia. pg. 2, Question 4, (2019)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Bolivia/Response%20by%20Bolivia.%20EN.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=4V9maU,
Bolivia,BOL,10. Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,11. Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,Silent,"“This statement is more genuinely justified when the “use of force” arises in a cyber-attack considered as an “armed attack,” on the understanding that a cyber virtual attack uses unconventional means that have the same impact an armed attack. Accordingly, Article 51 of the UN Charter recognizes the right to self-defense.”","OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response by Bolivia. pg. 2, Question 4, (2019)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Bolivia/Response%20by%20Bolivia.%20EN.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=4V9maU,
Bolivia,BOL,12. Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,“...a cyber operation constitutes use of force and generates the right to legitimate defense...”,"OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response by Bolivia. pg. 2, Question 4, (2019)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Bolivia/Response%20by%20Bolivia.%20EN.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=4V9maU,
Bolivia,BOL,12(a) Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,12(b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,"the “use of force” arises in a cyber-attack considered as an “armed attack,” on the understanding that a cyber virtual attack uses unconventional means that have the same impact an armed attack. Accordingly, Article 51 of the UN Charter recognizes the right to self-defense","OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response by Bolivia. pg. 2, Question 4, (2019)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Bolivia/Response%20by%20Bolivia.%20EN.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=4V9maU,
Bolivia,BOL,12(c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,"12(d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,12(e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,12(f) Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,12(g) Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,"12(h) Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,13. Can cyber operations that individually fall below the threshold of an armed attack aggregate into an armed attack triggering the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,14. Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation intend to cause harms that qualify the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,15. Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation produce reasonably foreseeable harms qualifying the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,16. Can cyber operations causing harms sufficient to qualify the operation as an armed attack trigger a third State’s right of self-defense if it was not the target of those operations?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,17. Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Silent,,,,
Bolivia,BOL,17(a) Must a State exercising collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation(s) constituting an armed attack have the victim state request or consent to such actions?,Silent,,,,
