Brazil,BRA,1. Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,"¶ 3: “As stated in previous reports of the United Nations Group of Governmental Experts on Developments in the Field of Information and Telecommunications in the Context of International Security, international law is applicable to the use of ICTs by States. This includes the legal prohibition of the use of force in international relations, which is enshrined in the UN Charter and is also part of customary international law. It is a peremptory norm,19 to which only two exceptions are permitted: self-defense and authorization under Chapter VII of the Charter.”  ¶ 1: “Amongst the gravest forms of the use of force in international relations are armed attacks, which trigger the right of states to resort to self-defense, in accordance with article 51 of the UN Charter. Being self-defense an exception to the general principle on the prohibition to the use of force, it needs to be interpreted restrictively”",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contribution,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Brazil/UNGA%20Official%20Compendium%20of%20Voluntary%20National%20Contributions(2021).pdf?CID=b36d6b07-0f1d-4d0a-a113-a36611671044,
Brazil,BRA,2. Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Yes,"¶ 1: “Amongst the gravest forms of the use of force in international relations are armed attacks, which trigger the right of states to resort to self-defense, in accordance with article 51 of the UN Charter. Being self-defense an exception to the general principle on the prohibition to the use of force, it needs to be interpreted restrictively”","UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contribution, pg. 20, ¶ 5, (2021)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Brazil/UNGA%20Official%20Compendium%20of%20Voluntary%20National%20Contributions(2021).pdf?CID=b36d6b07-0f1d-4d0a-a113-a36611671044,
Brazil,BRA,2(a) Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter,Silent,,,,
Brazil,BRA,3. Does a state’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,No,"“Self-defense is only triggered by an armed attack undertaken by or attributable to a State. It is not possible to invoke self-defense as a response to acts by non-State actors, unless they are acting on behalf or under the effective control of a state.”","UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contribution, pg. 20, ¶ 2, (2021)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Brazil/UNGA%20Official%20Compendium%20of%20Voluntary%20National%20Contributions(2021).pdf?CID=b36d6b07-0f1d-4d0a-a113-a36611671044,
Brazil,BRA,4. Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,Yes,"“...self-defense is only triggered by an armed attack undertaken by or attributable to a State.” “The definition of “armed attack” is limited to the use of force attributable to a state and, therefore, actions from non-state actors with similar effects might amount to serious crimes, but not an “armed attack”.”","UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contribution, pg. 20, ¶ 2,3, (2021)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Brazil/UNGA%20Official%20Compendium%20of%20Voluntary%20National%20Contributions(2021).pdf?CID=b36d6b07-0f1d-4d0a-a113-a36611671044,
Brazil,BRA,4(a) Can a State  exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,No,"“In the same vein, contemporary international law does not allow for self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non state actors whose cyber acts have extraterritorial effects.”","UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contribution, pg. 20, ¶ 3, (2021)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Brazil/UNGA%20Official%20Compendium%20of%20Voluntary%20National%20Contributions(2021).pdf?CID=b36d6b07-0f1d-4d0a-a113-a36611671044,
Brazil,BRA,5. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Yes,"“As with responses to armed activities using conventional weapons, self-defense against armed attacks caused by digital means must be necessary and proportionate.”","UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contribution, pg. 20, ¶ 5, (2021)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Brazil/UNGA%20Official%20Compendium%20of%20Voluntary%20National%20Contributions(2021).pdf?CID=b36d6b07-0f1d-4d0a-a113-a36611671044,
Brazil,BRA,5(a) Must force be the only option available to successfully repel and armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Brazil,BRA,6. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Yes,"“As with responses to armed activities using conventional weapons, self-defense against armed attacks caused by digital means must be necessary and proportionate.”","UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contribution, pg. 20, ¶ 5, (2021)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Brazil/UNGA%20Official%20Compendium%20of%20Voluntary%20National%20Contributions(2021).pdf?CID=b36d6b07-0f1d-4d0a-a113-a36611671044,
Brazil,BRA,7. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Yes,"“For Brazil, the right to self-defense exists once there is an actual or imminent armed attack.”","UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contribution, pg. 20, ¶ 5, (2021)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Brazil/UNGA%20Official%20Compendium%20of%20Voluntary%20National%20Contributions(2021).pdf?CID=b36d6b07-0f1d-4d0a-a113-a36611671044,
Brazil,BRA,8. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Silent,,,,
Brazil,BRA,9. Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,Yes,"“For Brazil, the right to self-defense exists once there is an actual or imminent armed attack. Under international law, there is no right to “preventive self-defense” - a notion that does not find legal grounds neither in art. 51 of the Charter nor in customary international law.”","UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contribution, pg. 20, ¶ 5, (2021)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Brazil/UNGA%20Official%20Compendium%20of%20Voluntary%20National%20Contributions(2021).pdf?CID=b36d6b07-0f1d-4d0a-a113-a36611671044,
Brazil,BRA,10. Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,No,"“Under international law, there is no right to “preventive self-defense” - a notion that does not find legal grounds neither in art. 51 of the Charter nor in customary international law.”","UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contribution, pg. 20, ¶ 5, (2021)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Brazil/UNGA%20Official%20Compendium%20of%20Voluntary%20National%20Contributions(2021).pdf?CID=b36d6b07-0f1d-4d0a-a113-a36611671044,
Brazil,BRA,11. Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,Silent,“Cyber operations may amount to an illegal use of force if they are attributable to a State and if their impact is similar to the impact of a kinetic attack.”,"UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contribution, pg. 19, ¶ 5, (2021)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Brazil/UNGA%20Official%20Compendium%20of%20Voluntary%20National%20Contributions(2021).pdf?CID=b36d6b07-0f1d-4d0a-a113-a36611671044,
