Chile,CHL,7. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Silent,,,,
Chile,CHL,8. Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Silent,,,,
Chile,CHL,9. Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Chile,CHL,10. Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Chile,CHL,11. Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,No,"“...it is not the means used but rather the effects on a state as a result of such an operation, cyber or kinetic, that determine whether there is use of force.”","OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response Submitted by Chile, pg. 1, Question 3",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Chile/Response%20by%20Chile.%20EN%20(1).DOCX?d=w88cf4e2dd83649f199b19e1fe85fe80c&csf=1&web=1&e=zsyWB2,
Chile,CHL,12. Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"the Republic of Chile has articulated its view that a cyberattack can be as harmful as an armed attack. Accordingly, the Republic of Chile is of the opinion that cyberattacks directed against its sovereignty, its inhabitants, its physical or information infrastructure, or cyber-attacks which severely affect its interests and satisfy the threshold of severity necessary as well as the requirements established by international law to be an armed attack, will give rise, in accordance with Article 51 of the United Nations Charter, to the right to self-defense, which may be exercised through physical means as well through cyberspace.","OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response Submitted by Chile, pg. 1, Question 3",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Chile/Response%20by%20Chile.%20EN%20(1).DOCX?d=w88cf4e2dd83649f199b19e1fe85fe80c&csf=1&web=1&e=zsyWB2,
Chile,CHL,12(a) Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Silent,,,,
Chile,CHL,12(b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Ambiguous,"“A cyber operation may therefore constitute a use of force per se in those cases in which its scale and effects are comparable to those of a cyber or non-cyber operation rising to the level of use of force.” “[C]yberattacks directed against its sovereignty, its inhabitants, its physical or information infrastructure, or cyber-attacks which severely affect its interests and satisfy the threshold of severity necessary as well as the requirements established by international law to be an armed attack, will give rise, in accordance with Article 51 of the United Nations Charter, to the right to self-defense""","OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response Submitted by Chile, pg. 1, Question 3",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Chile/Response%20by%20Chile.%20EN%20(1).DOCX?d=w88cf4e2dd83649f199b19e1fe85fe80c&csf=1&web=1&e=zsyWB2,
Chile,CHL,12(c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Ambiguous,"Chile is of the opinion that cyberattacks directed against its sovereignty, its inhabitants, its physical or information infrastructure, or cyber-attacks which severely affect its interests and satisfy the threshold of severity necessary as well as the requirements established by international law to be an armed attack, will give rise, in accordance with Article 51 of the United Nations Charter, to the right to self-defense, which may be exercised through physical means as well through cyberspace","OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response Submitted by Chile, pg. 1, Question 3",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Chile/Response%20by%20Chile.%20EN%20(1).DOCX?d=w88cf4e2dd83649f199b19e1fe85fe80c&csf=1&web=1&e=zsyWB2,
Chile,CHL,"12(d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
Chile,CHL,12(e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Chile,CHL,12(f) Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Chile,CHL,12(g) Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
Chile,CHL,"12(h) Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
Chile,CHL,13. Can cyber operations that individually fall below the threshold of an armed attack aggregate into an armed attack triggering the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Chile,CHL,14. Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation intend to cause harms that qualify the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Chile,CHL,15. Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation produce reasonably foreseeable harms qualifying the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Chile,CHL,16. Can cyber operations causing harms sufficient to qualify the operation as an armed attack trigger a third State’s right of self-defense if it was not the target of those operations?,Silent,,,,
Chile,CHL,17. Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Silent,,,,
Chile,CHL,17(a) Must a State exercising collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation(s) constituting an armed attack have the victim state request or consent to such actions?,Silent,,,,
Chile,CHL,"18. In its response to a cyber operation or operations qualifying as an armed attack, is the injured state limited to cyber means in response?",No,"“Accordingly, the Republic of Chile is of the opinion that cyberattacks directed against its sovereignty, its inhabitants, its physical or information infrastructure, or cyber-attacks which severely affect its interests and satisfy the threshold of severity necessary as well as the requirements established by international law to be an armed attack, will give rise, in accordance with Article 51 of the United Nations Charter, to the right to self-defense, which may be exercised through physical means as well through cyberspace.”","OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response Submitted by Chile, pg. 1, Question 3",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Chile/Response%20by%20Chile.%20EN%20(1).DOCX?d=w88cf4e2dd83649f199b19e1fe85fe80c&csf=1&web=1&e=zsyWB2,
Chile,CHL,19. Must a State employing the right of self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack report its actions to the UN Security Council?,Silent,,,,
China,CHN,1. Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,"“The UN Charter and the principles enshrined in it, including sovereign equality, refraining from the use or threat of force, settlement of international disputes by peaceful means and non-intervention in the internal affairs of other States, apply in cyberspace. The application of these principles is the cornerstone of the peace, security and stability in cyberspace.”","China’s Positions on International Rules-making in Cyberspace, pg. 2, ¶ 3, (2021)",https://perma.cc/G2JV-C5FF,
