Colombia,COL,1. Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,"It is the view of Colombia that, when exercising the right to self-defense in response to a cyber activity or operation that amounts to an armed attack, States must uphold the thresholds and limitations outlined in Article 51.","Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 15, § 12.1 (2025)",https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,2. Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Yes,"“A State targeted by a cyber activity or operation that amounts to an armed attack may invoke its inherent right of self-defense, pursuant to the customary right to self-defense, as outlined in Article 51 of the UN Charter and reflected in Article 21 ARSIWA[22].”","Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 15, § 12.1 (2025)",https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,2(a) Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter,Yes,"“A State targeted by a cyber activity or operation that amounts to an armed attack may invoke its inherent right of self-defense, pursuant to the customary right to self-defense, as outlined in Article 51 of the UN Charter and reflected in Article 21 ARSIWA[22].”","Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 15, § 12.1 (2025)",https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
