Colombia,COL,15. Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation produce reasonably foreseeable harms qualifying the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Colombia,COL,16. Can cyber operations causing harms sufficient to qualify the operation as an armed attack trigger a third State’s right of self-defense if it was not the target of those operations?,Silent,,,,
Colombia,COL,17. Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Yes,“Nothing in the present Charter shall impair the inherent right of individual or collective self-defense if an armed attack occurs against a Member of the United Nations...”,"Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 20, FOOTNOTES (2025)",https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,17(a) Must a State exercising collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation(s) constituting an armed attack have the victim state request or consent to such actions?,Silent,,,,
Colombia,COL,"18. In its response to a cyber operation or operations qualifying as an armed attack, is the injured state limited to cyber means in response?",Silent,,,,
Colombia,COL,19. Must a State employing the right of self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack report its actions to the UN Security Council?,Yes,“Measures taken by Members in the exercise of this right of self-defence shall be immediately reported to the Security Council and shall not in any way affect the authority and responsibility of the Security Council under the present Charter to take at any time such action as it deems necessary in order to maintain or restore international peace and security.”,"Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 20, FOOTNOTES (2025)",https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Costa Rica,CRI,1)Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,"“In accordance with Article 51 of the UN Charter and customary international law, an armed attack triggers the right of States to exercise individual or collective self-defense.""","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 11, § 37,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,2)Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Yes,"“In accordance with Article 51 of the UN Charter and customary international law, an armed attack triggers the right of States to exercise individual or collective self-defense""","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 11, § 37,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
