Costa Rica,CRI,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,“Examples of cyber operations potentially constituting armed attacks are those causing significant loss of life and destruction of critical infrastructure.”,"Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 11, § 37,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Yes,""" For Costa Rica, this assessment is to be carried out on the basis of a comparison between the scale and effects of a cyber operation and those of an operation by conventional weapons that would constitute an armed attack.” § 36: “Although this assessment can only be carried out on a case-by-case basis, examples of cyber operations likely amounting to a prohibited use of force include those causing physical harm to individuals or significant destruction of property, as well as those permanently disabling operating systems controlling critical infrastructure, such as an electrical grid or a water and sanitation station.”","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 11, § 36, 37,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,""" As noted by the ICJ, armed attacks are the ‘most grave’ forms of use of force. For Costa Rica, this assessment is to be carried out on the basis of a comparison between the scale and effects of a cyber operation and those of an operation by conventional weapons that would constitute an armed attack”","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 11, § 37,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,12c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Yes,"“As noted by the ICJ, armed attacks are the ‘most grave’ forms of use of force. For Costa Rica, this assessment is to be carried out on the basis of a comparison between the scale and effects...”","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 11, § 37,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,"12d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",Ambiguous,“Examples of cyber operations potentially constituting armed attacks are those causing significant loss of life and destruction of critical infrastructure.”,"Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 11, § 37,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,12e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,No,"“A prohibited threat or use of force must be distinguished from an armed attack. In accordance with Article 51 of the UN Charter and customary international law, an armed attack triggers the right of States to exercise individual or collective self-defense"" armed attacks are the ‘most grave’ forms of use of force.","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 11, § 37,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,12f)Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Yes,"“In Costa Rica’s view, to give rise to the right of self-defense, an armed attack must be attributable to a State, in cyberspace as in any other context. As noted by the ICJ, armed attacks are the ‘most grave’ forms of use of force. For Costa Rica, this assessment is to be carried out on the basis of a comparison between the scale and effects of a cyber operation and those of an operation by conventional weapons that would constitute an armed attack.”","Costa Rica’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 11, § 37,",https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
