Cuba,CUB,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,No,"“Under current international law, a cyber action does not constitute an armed attack in accordance with Article 2(4), since it lacks the physical characteristics and other requirements that define the aforementioned military attacks, their effects and legal consequences”",Position Paper of the Republic of Cuba on the Application of International Law to Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Cuba/Spanish%20to%20English%20Translation%20of%20Cuba%20National%20Position%20(2024).docx?d=w1ab3da0163084f429ac48954540bb9c8&csf=1&web=1&e=gBGGLM,
Cuba,CUB,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Silent,,,,
Cuba,CUB,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
