Cuba,CUB,12e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,No,"“Under current international law, a cyber action does not constitute an armed attack in accordance with Article 2(4), since it lacks the physical characteristics and other requirements that define the aforementioned military attacks, their effects and legal consequences”",Position Paper of the Republic of Cuba on the Application of International Law to Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Cuba/Spanish%20to%20English%20Translation%20of%20Cuba%20National%20Position%20(2024).docx?d=w1ab3da0163084f429ac48954540bb9c8&csf=1&web=1&e=gBGGLM,
Cuba,CUB,12f)Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Cuba,CUB,12g)Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,No,"It is politically dangerous and legally premature to consider that an improper use of ICTs could be equated to the use of force for the purpose of triggering the recourse to self-defense, when this is exclusively limited under Article 51 of the UN Charter to cases of armed attack.",Position Paper of the Republic of Cuba on the Application of International Law to Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Cuba/Spanish%20to%20English%20Translation%20of%20Cuba%20National%20Position%20(2024).docx?d=w1ab3da0163084f429ac48954540bb9c8&csf=1&web=1&e=gBGGLM,
Cuba,CUB,"12h)Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",No,"It is politically dangerous and legally premature to consider that an improper use of ICTs could be equated to the use of force for the purpose of triggering the recourse to self-defense, when this is exclusively limited under Article 51 of the UN Charter to cases of armed attack.",Position Paper of the Republic of Cuba on the Application of International Law to Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Cuba/Spanish%20to%20English%20Translation%20of%20Cuba%20National%20Position%20(2024).docx?d=w1ab3da0163084f429ac48954540bb9c8&csf=1&web=1&e=gBGGLM,
Cuba,CUB,13)Can cyber operations that individually fall below the threshold of an armed attack aggregate into an armed attack triggering the right of self-defense?,No,"It is politically dangerous and legally premature to consider that an improper use of ICTs could be equated to the use of force for the purpose of triggering the recourse to self-defense, when this is exclusively limited under Article 51 of the UN Charter to cases of armed attack.",Position Paper of the Republic of Cuba on the Application of International Law to Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Cuba/Spanish%20to%20English%20Translation%20of%20Cuba%20National%20Position%20(2024).docx?d=w1ab3da0163084f429ac48954540bb9c8&csf=1&web=1&e=gBGGLM,
