Czech Republic,CZE,1)Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,"“The Czech Republic reiterates the provisions of the UN Charter and customary international law that if an armed attack occurs, the affected State may exercise its inherent right to individual or collective self-defence under Article 51 of the UN Charter.”",Position Paper of Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,2)Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Yes,"“The Czech Republic reiterates the provisions of the UN Charter and customary international law that if an armed attack occurs, the affected State may exercise its inherent right to individual or collective self-defence under Article 51 of the UN Charter.”",Position Paper of the Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,2a) Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter,Silent,"“The Czech Republic reiterates the provisions of the UN Charter and customary international law that if an armed attack occurs, the affected State may exercise its inherent right to individual or collective self-defense under Article 51 of the UN Charter.” “The exercise of the right to self-defense shall meet the conditions of necessity and proportionality, but is not limited to cyber means and cyber domain, even if the armed attack was so conducted.",Position Paper of the Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,3)Does a State’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,4)Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,4a)Can a State  exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,5)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Yes,“The exercise of the right to self-defense shall meet the conditions of necessity and proportionality”,Position Paper of the Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,5a)Must force be the only option available to successfully repel an armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,6)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Yes,“The exercise of the right to self-defense shall meet the conditions of necessity and proportionality”,Position Paper of the Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,7)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,8)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,9)Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,10)Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,11)Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,No,"“The prohibition of the threat or use of force contained in the UN Charter is deliberately general and encompasses any means and methods used (whether kinetic or cyber means). Thus, the prohibition of the threat or use of force applies also in cyberspace.",Position Paper of the Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"“The exercise of the right to self-defence shall meet the conditions of necessity and proportionality, but is not limited to cyber means and cyber domain, even if the armed attack was so conducted.”",Position Paper of the Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,""" If a cyber operation was comparable in its scale and effect to an attack by conventional means (kinetic operations) in terms of its gravity, such as fatalities, damage and destruction, it could be considered as constituting an armed attack under Article 51 of the UN  Charter.",Position Paper of the Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,12c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Yes,“In accordance with the general guidance provided by the International Court of Justice there are two main factors to be considered when assessing whether a use of force constitutes an armed attack: the scale and effects of the particular act...”,Position Paper of the Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,"12d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",Ambiguous,"If a cyber operation was comparable in its scale and effect to an attack by conventional means (kinetic operations) in terms of its gravity, such as fatalities, damage  and destruction, it could be considered as constituting an armed attack under Article 51 of the UN  Charter",Position Paper of the Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,12e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,No,"The term ""use of force"" is generally perceived to be a  broader concept than ""armed attack"", which is considered as the most grave form of the use of  force",Position Paper of the Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,12f)Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Yes,"The term ""use of force"" is generally perceived to be a  broader concept than ""armed attack"", which is considered as the most grave form of the use of  force",Position Paper of the Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,12g)Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,"12h)Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,13)Can cyber operations that individually fall below the threshold of an armed attack aggregate into an armed attack triggering the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,14)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation intend to cause harms that qualify the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,15)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation produce reasonably foreseeable harms qualifying the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,16)Can cyber operations causing harms sufficient to qualify the operation as an armed attack trigger a third State’s right of self-defense if it was not the target of those operations?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,17)Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Yes,“Collective self-defence against a cyber operation amounting to an armed attack can be exercised only at the request of the victim State and within the scope of such a request.”,Position Paper of the Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,17a)Must a State exercising collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation(s) constituting an armed attack have the victim state request or consent to such actions?,Yes,“Collective self-defence against a cyber operation amounting to an armed attack can be exercised only at the request of the victim State and within the scope of such a request.”,Position Paper of the Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,"18)In its response to a cyber operation or operations qualifying as an armed attack, is the injured state limited to cyber means in response?",No,"“The exercise of the right to self-defense... is not limited to cyber means and cyber domain, even if the armed attack was so conducted.”",Position Paper of the Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,19)Must a State employing the right of self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack report its actions to the UN Security Council?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,1)Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,"“Denmark takes the view that a cyber-attack may qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action, which would otherwise qualify as an armed attack”","Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Denmark/Jul.%202023%20Denmark%20position%20paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=g3DPvQ,
Denmark,DNK,2)Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Silent,,"Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Denmark/Jul.%202023%20Denmark%20position%20paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=g3DPvQ,
Denmark,DNK,2a)Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,3)Does a State’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,Yes,"“Denmark does not share this view, but contends that State practice supports that a State might in some instances and under certain conditions be permitted to exercise self-defence against an armed attack by a non-State actor.”","Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Denmark/Jul.%202023%20Denmark%20position%20paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=g3DPvQ,
Denmark,DNK,4)Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,No,“Certain States take the view that an armed attack can only be undertaken by State actors or entities acting under the control or instruction of States... Denmark does not share this view.”,"Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Denmark/Jul.%202023%20Denmark%20position%20paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=g3DPvQ,
Denmark,DNK,4a)Can a State  exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,5)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,5a)Must force be the only option available to successfully repel and armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,6)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,7)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,8)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,9)Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,10)Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,11)Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,Silent,,"Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Denmark/Jul.%202023%20Denmark%20position%20paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=g3DPvQ,
Denmark,DNK,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"“Denmark takes the view that a cyber attack may qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action, which would otherwise qualify as an armed attack”","Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Denmark/Jul.%202023%20Denmark%20position%20paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=g3DPvQ,
Denmark,DNK,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Silent,,"Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Denmark/Jul.%202023%20Denmark%20position%20paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=g3DPvQ,
Denmark,DNK,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,"“Denmark takes the view that a cyber attack may qualify... if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action, which would otherwise qualify as an armed attack”  “Thus, Denmark considers that a cyber operation, which e.g. leads to serious injury or death, or which causes significant physical damage, may qualify as an armed attack”","Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Denmark/Jul.%202023%20Denmark%20position%20paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=g3DPvQ,
Denmark,DNK,12c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Yes,"It has been suggested that States should apply the following non-exhaustive  factors for determining if a cyber operation reaches the level of use of force:  Severity, immediacy, directness, invasiveness, measurability of effects, military  character, State involvement, presumptive legality.5 While few States in their  public positions have endorsed these particular factors, Denmark is of the  view that these factors are useful reference points for further understanding  and discussing the definition of use of force in cyberspace. In certain instances, use of force may due to its scale and effects reach the  level of an armed attack and thus give rise to a right to self-defence of the  target State, cf. article 51 of the UN Charter.","Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Denmark/Jul.%202023%20Denmark%20position%20paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=g3DPvQ,
Denmark,DNK,"12d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",Ambiguous,"“Denmark considers that a cyber operation, which e.g. leads to serious injury or death, or which causes significant physical damage, may qualify as an armed attack.”","Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Denmark/Jul.%202023%20Denmark%20position%20paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=g3DPvQ,
Denmark,DNK,12e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,No,“Denmark subscribes to the understanding that not all illegal use of force under article 2(4) of the UN Charter necessarily amounts to an armed attack under article 51 of the Charter”,"Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Denmark/Jul.%202023%20Denmark%20position%20paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=g3DPvQ,
Denmark,DNK,12f)Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Yes,“In its Nicaragua judgment the ICJ defined an armed attack as the most grave form of the use of force.[6] Denmark subscribes to the understanding that not all illegal use of force... necessarily amounts to an armed attack under article 51 of the Charter.”,"Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Denmark/Jul.%202023%20Denmark%20position%20paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=g3DPvQ,
Denmark,DNK,12g)Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,Ambiguous,"With regard to the precise interpretation of the term force and the question as to whether economic or political coercion can qualify as use of force, Denmark considers that it generally cannot be ruled out that acts of economic or political coercion can fall within the purview of Article 2(4) of the UN Charter if, for example, a cyber operation resulting in the malfunctioning of a State’s financial system leads to significant economic damage.","Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Denmark/Jul.%202023%20Denmark%20position%20paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=g3DPvQ,
Denmark,DNK,"12h)Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",Ambiguous,"With regard to the precise interpretation of the term force and the question as to whether economic or political coercion can qualify as use of force, Denmark considers that it generally cannot be ruled out that acts of economic or political coercion can fall within the purview of Article 2(4) of the UN Charter if, for example, a cyber operation resulting in the malfunctioning of a State’s financial system leads to significant economic damage.","Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Denmark/Jul.%202023%20Denmark%20position%20paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=g3DPvQ,
Denmark,DNK,13)Can cyber operations that individually fall below the threshold of an armed attack aggregate into an armed attack triggering the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,14)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation intend to cause harms that qualify the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,15)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation produce reasonably foreseeable harms qualifying the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,16)Can cyber operations causing harms sufficient to qualify the operation as an armed attack trigger a third State’s right of self-defense if it was not the target of those operations?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,17)Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,17a)Must a State exercising collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation(s) constituting an armed attack have the victim state request or consent to such actions?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,"18)In its response to a cyber operation or operations qualifying as an armed attack, is the injured state limited to cyber means in response?",Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,19)Must a State employing the right of self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack report its actions to the UN Security Council?,Silent,,,,
Ecuador,ECU,1)Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,yes,"The individual or collective right to self-defense is set forth in Article 51 of the UN Charter, which states: 'Nothing in the present Charter shall impair the inherent right of individual or collective self-defense if an armed attack occurs against a Member of the United Nations, until the Security Council has taken measures necessary to maintain international peace and security.'",OAS Survey Response Submitted by Ecuador,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/s/iLITHome/IQCnwd0Jqcv_QqwRRxQs5NbrAcGo4Q-lORKcLhHJ8y3ol3w?email=michellexin%40temple.edu&e=M43Dw5,
Ecuador,ECU,2)Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Silent,,,,
