Estonia,EST,8)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Silent,,,,
Estonia,EST,9)Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Estonia,EST,10)Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Estonia,EST,11)Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
Estonia,EST,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"“In order to assess if a cyber operation reaches the threshold of the use of force or an armed attack based on Article 2(4) or 51 of the Un Charter, we must consider the scale and effects of the operation. If the effects of a cyber operation are comparable to a kinetic attack, it could constitute an armed attack.”",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary Nat’l Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Estonia/UNGA%20Official%20Compendium%20of%20Voluntary%20National%20Contributions%20(2021).pdf,
Estonia,EST,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Silent,,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary Nat’l Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Estonia/UNGA%20Official%20Compendium%20of%20Voluntary%20National%20Contributions%20(2021).pdf,
Estonia,EST,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,"“In order to assess if a cyber operation reaches the threshold of the use of force or an armed attack based on Article 2(4) or 51 of the Un Charter, we must consider the scale and effects of the operation. If the effects of a cyber operation are comparable to a kinetic attack, it could constitute an armed attack”",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary Nat’l Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Estonia/UNGA%20Official%20Compendium%20of%20Voluntary%20National%20Contributions%20(2021).pdf,
Estonia,EST,12c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Yes,"“In order to assess if a cyber operation reaches the threshold of the use of force or an armed attack based on Article 2(4) or 51 of the Un Charter, we must consider the scale and effects of the operation.”",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary Nat’l Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Estonia/UNGA%20Official%20Compendium%20of%20Voluntary%20National%20Contributions%20(2021).pdf,
