European Union,EUN,1)Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,"“A State targeted by a cyber operation constituting an armed attack may invoke its inherent right of individual or collective self-defence, in accordance with the requirements for the exercise of that right laid down in Article 51 of the UN Charter.”",EU Council Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/European%20Union/EU%20Council%20Declaration%20on%20a%20Common%20Understanding%20of%20International%20Law%20in%20Cyberspace%20(2024).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=TiNbZc,
European Union,EUN,2)Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,2a)Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,3)Does a State’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,4)Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,4a)Can a State  exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,5)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Yes,"In order to constitute a lawful use of force in the exercise of individual or collective self-defence, the use of armed force in self-defence must comply with the requirements of necessity and proportionality",EU Council Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/European%20Union/EU%20Council%20Declaration%20on%20a%20Common%20Understanding%20of%20International%20Law%20in%20Cyberspace%20(2024).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=TiNbZc,
European Union,EUN,5a)Must force be the only option available to successfully repel and armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,6)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Yes,"In order to constitute a lawful use of force in the exercise of individual or collective self-defence, the use of armed force in self-defence must comply with the requirements of necessity and proportionality",EU Council Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/European%20Union/EU%20Council%20Declaration%20on%20a%20Common%20Understanding%20of%20International%20Law%20in%20Cyberspace%20(2024).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=TiNbZc,
European Union,EUN,7)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,8)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,9)Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,Silent,,"EU Council Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/European%20Union/EU%20Council%20Declaration%20on%20a%20Common%20Understanding%20of%20International%20Law%20in%20Cyberspace%20(2024).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=TiNbZc,
European Union,EUN,10)Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,Silent,"Yeah, yeah","EU Council Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/European%20Union/EU%20Council%20Declaration%20on%20a%20Common%20Understanding%20of%20International%20Law%20in%20Cyberspace%20(2024).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=TiNbZc,
European Union,EUN,11)Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"“A State targeted by a cyber operation constituting an armed attack may invoke its inherent right of individual or collective self-defence, in accordance with the requirements for the exercise of that right laid down in Article 51 of the UN Charter.”",EU Council Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/European%20Union/EU%20Council%20Declaration%20on%20a%20Common%20Understanding%20of%20International%20Law%20in%20Cyberspace%20(2024).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=TiNbZc,
European Union,EUN,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,"In order for a cyber operation to constitute an armed attack, the scale and effects of the operation must be comparable to those of a conventional kinetic attack.",EU Council Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/European%20Union/EU%20Council%20Declaration%20on%20a%20Common%20Understanding%20of%20International%20Law%20in%20Cyberspace%20(2024).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=TiNbZc,
European Union,EUN,12c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Yes,"The extent of any damage or destruction of property (including ICT infrastructure) or injury or death of persons, including as an indirect effect, are among the relevant factors for assessing whether the scale and effects of the cyber operation are grave enough to characterise it as an armed attack.",EU Council Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/European%20Union/EU%20Council%20Declaration%20on%20a%20Common%20Understanding%20of%20International%20Law%20in%20Cyberspace%20(2024).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=TiNbZc,
European Union,EUN,"12d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",Ambiguous,"The extent of any damage or destruction of property (including ICT infrastructure) or injury or death of persons, including as an indirect effect, are among the relevant factors for assessing whether the scale and effects of the cyber operation are grave enough to characterise it as an armed attack.",EU Council Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/European%20Union/EU%20Council%20Declaration%20on%20a%20Common%20Understanding%20of%20International%20Law%20in%20Cyberspace%20(2024).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=TiNbZc,
European Union,EUN,12e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,No.,"Moreover, the use of force does not always  constitute an armed attack.",EU Council Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/European%20Union/EU%20Council%20Declaration%20on%20a%20Common%20Understanding%20of%20International%20Law%20in%20Cyberspace%20(2024).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=TiNbZc,
European Union,EUN,12f)Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Yes,"In order for a cyber operation to constitute an armed attack, the scale and effects of the operation must be comparable to those of a conventional kinetic attack. The extent of any damage or destruction of property (including ICT infrastructure) or injury or death of persons, including as an indirect effect, are among the relevant factors for assessing whether the scale and effects of the cyber operation are grave enough to characterise it as an armed attack.",EU Council Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/European%20Union/EU%20Council%20Declaration%20on%20a%20Common%20Understanding%20of%20International%20Law%20in%20Cyberspace%20(2024).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=TiNbZc,
European Union,EUN,12g) Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,"12h)Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,13)Can cyber operations that individually fall below the threshold of an armed attack aggregate into an armed attack triggering the right of self-defense?,Ambiguous,"“Depending on the circumstances, the combined effects of several cyber operations could, taken  together, be comparable to a kinetic use of force.”",EU Council Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/European%20Union/EU%20Council%20Declaration%20on%20a%20Common%20Understanding%20of%20International%20Law%20in%20Cyberspace%20(2024).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=TiNbZc,
European Union,EUN,14)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation intend to cause harms that qualify the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,15)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation produce reasonably foreseeable harms qualifying the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,16)Can cyber operations causing harms sufficient to qualify the operation as an armed attack trigger a third State’s right of self-defense if it was not the target of those operations?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,17)Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Yes,"When a State invokes the right of self-defence in response to an armed attack, it may ask other States to act in its support or on its behalf. The right of self-defence may thus be exercised collectively but only at the specific request of the victim State.",EU Council Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/European%20Union/EU%20Council%20Declaration%20on%20a%20Common%20Understanding%20of%20International%20Law%20in%20Cyberspace%20(2024).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=TiNbZc,
European Union,EUN,17a)Must a State exercising collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation(s) constituting an armed attack have the victim state request or consent to such actions?,Yes,"When a State invokes the right of self-defence in response to an armed attack, it may ask other States to act in its support or on its behalf. The right of self-defence may thus be exercised collectively but only at the specific request of the victim State",EU Council Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/European%20Union/EU%20Council%20Declaration%20on%20a%20Common%20Understanding%20of%20International%20Law%20in%20Cyberspace%20(2024).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=TiNbZc,
European Union,EUN,"18)In its response to a cyber operation or operations qualifying as an armed attack, is the injured state limited to cyber means in response?",Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,19)Must a State employing the right of self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack report its actions to the UN Security Council?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,1)Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,"“While there is currently no established definition of a cyberattack that would pass the threshold “of “use of force” in the sense of article 2(4) of the UN Charter, or “armed attack” in the sense of article 51, it is widely recognized that such a qualification depends on the consequences of a Cyberattack.""",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Finland/Finland%20Statements.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=IeB5Tf,
Finland,FIN,2)Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,2a)Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,3)Does a State’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,No,“The right of self-defence arises if a cyberattack comparable to an armed attack occurs and can be attributed to a particular State”,International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Finland/Finland%20Statements.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=IeB5Tf,
Finland,FIN,4)Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,Yes,“The right of self-defence arises if a cyberattack comparable to an armed attack occurs and can be attributed to a particular State”,International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Finland/Finland%20Statements.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=IeB5Tf,
Finland,FIN,4a)Can a State  exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,5)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,5a)Must force be the only option available to successfully repel and armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,6)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Yes,"“At the same time, the use of force must not be disproportionate or excessive.”",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Finland/Finland%20Statements.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=IeB5Tf,
Finland,FIN,7)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,8)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,9)Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,10)Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,11)Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"“Similarly, most commentators agree that when the scale and effects of a cyberattack correspond to those of an armed attack responding to the cyberattack is justifiable as self-defence.”",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Finland/Finland%20Statements.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=IeB5Tf,
Finland,FIN,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Yes,"“It is obvious that the attack must have caused death, injury or substantial material damage, but it is impossible to set a precise quantitative threshold for the effects, and other circumstantial factors must be taken into account in the analysis, as well.”",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Finland/Finland%20Statements.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=IeB5Tf,
Finland,FIN,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,"“Similarly, most commentators agree that when the scale and effects of a cyberattack correspond to those of an armed attack responding to the cyberattack is justifiable as self-defence.”",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Finland/Finland%20Statements.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=IeB5Tf,
Finland,FIN,12c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,No,"“It is obvious that the attack must have caused death, injury or substantial material damage, but it is impossible to set a precise quantitative threshold for the effects, and other circumstantial factors must be taken into account in the analysis, as well.”",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Finland/Finland%20Statements.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=IeB5Tf,
Finland,FIN,"12d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",Yes,"“It is obvious that the attack must have caused death, injury or substantial material damage, but it is impossible to set a precise quantitative threshold for the effects, and other circumstantial factors must be taken into account in the analysis, as well.”  “Similarly, most commentators agree that when the scale and effects of a cyberattack correspond to those of an armed attack responding to the cyberattack is justifiable as self-defence.”",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Finland/Finland%20Statements.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=IeB5Tf,
Finland,FIN,12e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,No,"This would mean, for instance, that the distinction between armed attack as a  particularly serious violation of the Charter, on the one hand, and any lesser uses of force, on the other, is preserved.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Finland/Finland%20Statements.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=IeB5Tf,
