Finland,FIN,"18)In its response to a cyber operation or operations qualifying as an armed attack, is the injured state limited to cyber means in response?",No,“It is reasonable to think that a State victim to such an attack can respond with either cyber means or armed action”,International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Finland/Finland%20Statements.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=IeB5Tf,
Finland,FIN,19)Must a State employing the right of self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack report its actions to the UN Security Council?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,1)Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,"“Under Article 51 of the United Nations Charter, a State that suffers an armed attack is entitled to use individual or collective self-defence""  Cyberattacks may constitute a grave form of the use of force to which France could respond by self-defence. The specific characteristics of cyberspace do not call into question France’s position with regard to self-defence in response to cyberattacks which reach the threshold of an armed attack within the meaning of Article 51 of the United Nations Charter",Position Paper of France on International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=esjo0U,
France,FRA,2)Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,2a)Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,3)Does a State’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,Ambiguous,"In accordance with ICJ case law, France does not recognise the extension of the right to self-defence to acts perpetrated by non-state actors whose actions are not attributable, directly or indirectly, to a State.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Finland/Finland%20Statements.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=IeB5Tf,
France,FRA,4)Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,Ambiguous,"To be categorised as an armed attack, a cyberattack must also have been perpetrated, directly or indirectly, by a State. Leaving aside acts perpetrated by persons belonging to State organs or exercising elements of governmental authority, a State is responsible for acts perpetrated by non-state actors only if they act de facto on its instructions or orders or under its control in accordance with the rules on State responsibility for internationally wrongful acts and ICJ case law.  . . .  France has, in exceptional cases, invoked self-defence against an armed attack perpetrated by an actor having the characteristics of a “quasi-State”, as with its intervention in Syria against the terrorist group Daesh (ISIS/ISIL)24. However, this exceptional case cannot constitute the definitive expression of recognition of the extension of the concept of self-defence to acts perpetrated by non-state actors acting without the direct or indirect support of a State. Nonetheless, it cannot be ruled out that general practice may shift towards an interpretation of the law of self-defence as being authorised in response to an armed attack by non-state actors whose acts are not attributable to a State. However, any such development will have to be made bearing in mind the Rome Statute of the International Criminal Court (ICC) as amended in 2010 to add the crime of aggression25, and the case law of the ICC that may emerge in this sphere26.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Finland/Finland%20Statements.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=IeB5Tf,
France,FRA,4a)Can a State  exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,5)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Yes,“Self-defence in response to an armed attack carried out in cyberspace may involve digital or conventional means in compliance with the principles of necessity and proportionality”,Position Paper of France on International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=esjo0U,
France,FRA,5a)Must force be the only option available to successfully repel and armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,6)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Yes,“Self-defence in response to an armed attack carried out in cyberspace may involve digital or conventional means in compliance with the principles of necessity and proportionality”,Position Paper of France on International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=esjo0U,
France,FRA,7)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Yes,"“In exceptional circumstances, France allows itself to use pre-emptive self-defence in response to a cyberattack that 'has not yet been triggered but is about to be, in an imminent and certain manner, provided that the potential impact of such an attack is sufficiently serious.'”","Position Paper of France on International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=esjo0U,
France,FRA,8)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,9)Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,Yes,"“In exceptional circumstances, France allows itself to use pre-emptive self-defence in response to a cyberattack that 'has not yet been triggered but is about to be, in an imminent and certain manner”",Position Paper of France on International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=esjo0U,
France,FRA,10)Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,No,"“However, it does not recognise the legality of the use of force on the grounds of preventive self-defence""",Position Paper of France on International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=esjo0U,
France,FRA,11)Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,“The specific characteristics of cyberspace do not call into question France’s position with regard to self-defence in response to cyberattacks which reach the threshold of an armed attack within the meaning of Article 51 of the United Nations Charter.”,Position Paper of France on International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=esjo0U,
France,FRA,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,"France reaffirms that a cyberattack may constitute an armed attack within the meaning of Article 51 of the United Nations Charter19, if it is of a scale and severity comparable to those resulting from the use of physical force  “The specific characteristics of cyberspace do not call into question France’s position with regard to self-defence in response to cyberattacks which reach the threshold of an armed attack within the meaning of Article 51 of the United Nations Charter”",Position Paper of France on International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace Position Paper of France on International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=esjo0U,
France,FRA,12c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Yes,“Cyberattacks which do not reach the threshold of an armed attack when taken in isolation could be categorised as such if the accumulation of their effects reaches a sufficient threshold of gravity”,"Position Paper of France on International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=esjo0U,
France,FRA,"12d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
France,FRA,12e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,No,"In accordance with the case law of the International Court of Justice (ICJ), France distinguishes the gravest forms of the use of force, which constitute an armed attack to which the victim State may respond by individual or collective self-defence, from other less grave forms",Position Paper of France on International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=esjo0U,
France,FRA,12f)Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Yes,"In accordance with the case law of the International Court of Justice (ICJ), France distinguishes the gravest forms of the use of force, which constitute an armed attack to which the victim State may respond by individual or collective self-defence, from other less grave forms1",Position Paper of France on International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=esjo0U,
France,FRA,12g)Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,"12h)Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
France,FRA,13)Can cyber operations that individually fall below the threshold of an armed attack aggregate into an armed attack triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,“Cyberattacks which do not reach the threshold of an armed attack when taken in isolation could be categorised as such if the accumulation of their effects reaches a sufficient threshold of gravity.”,Position Paper of France on International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=esjo0U,
France,FRA,14)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation intend to cause harms that qualify the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,15)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation produce reasonably foreseeable harms qualifying the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,16)Can cyber operations causing harms sufficient to qualify the operation as an armed attack trigger a third State’s right of self-defense if it was not the target of those operations?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,17)Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Yes,"France distinguishes the gravest forms of the use of force, which constitute an armed attack to which the victim State may respond by individual or collective self-defence, from other less grave forms",,,
France,FRA,17a)Must a State exercising collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation(s) constituting an armed attack have the victim state request or consent to such actions?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,"18)In its response to a cyber operation or operations qualifying as an armed attack, is the injured state limited to cyber means in response?",No,“Self-defence in response to an armed attack carried out in cyberspace may involve digital or conventional means”,Position Paper of France on International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=esjo0U,
France,FRA,19)Must a State employing the right of self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack report its actions to the UN Security Council?,Yes,"“Lastly, any response on the grounds of self-defence remains provisional and subordinate. It must be promptly reported to the UNSC”","France (2021), p.7, para. 4 Position Paper of France on International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=esjo0U,
Germany,DEU,1)Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,“The right to self-defence according to art. 51 UN Charter is triggered if an armed attack occurs. Malicious cyber operations can constitute an armed attack whenever they are comparable to traditional kinetic armed attack in scale and effect.”,"Position Paper of Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Germany/Germany_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=gvGjQ3,
Germany,DEU,2)Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,2a)Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,3)Does a State’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,Yes,“Acts of non-State actors can also constitute armed attacks. Germany has expressed this view both with regard to the attacks by Al Qaeda and the attacks of ISIS”,"Position Paper of Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Germany/Germany_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=gvGjQ3,
Germany,DEU,4)Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,No,“Acts of non-State actors can also constitute armed attacks.”,"Position Paper of Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Germany/Germany_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=gvGjQ3,
Germany,DEU,4a)Can a State  exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,5)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Yes,"“Once the right to self-defence is triggered, the State under attack can resort to all necessary and proportionate means in order to end the attack”","Position Paper of Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Germany/Germany_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=gvGjQ3,
Germany,DEU,5a)Must force be the only option available to successfully repel and armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,6)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Yes,"“Once the right to self-defence is triggered, the State under attack can resort to all necessary and proportionate means in order to end the attack”","Position Paper of Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Germany/Germany_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=gvGjQ3,
Germany,DEU,7)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Yes,"“In Germany’s view, art. 51 UN Charter requires the attack against which a State can resort to self-defence to be ‘imminent’. The same applies with regard to self-defence against malicious cyber operations.”","Position Paper of Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Germany/Germany_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=gvGjQ3,
Germany,DEU,8)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,9)Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,Ambiguous,"In Germany’s view, art. 51 UN Charter requires the attack against which a State can resort to self-defence to be ‘imminent’. The same applies with regard to self-defence against malicious cyber operations. Strikes against a prospective attacker who has not yet initiated an attack do not qualify as lawful self-defence","Position Paper of Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Germany/Germany_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=gvGjQ3,
Germany,DEU,10)Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,No,"“Strikes against a prospective attacker who has not yet initiated an attack do not qualify as lawful self-defence""","Position Paper of Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Germany/Germany_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=gvGjQ3,
Germany,DEU,11)Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,Silent,,"Position Paper of Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Germany/Germany_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=gvGjQ3,
Germany,DEU,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,“Malicious cyber operations can constitute an armed attack whenever they are comparable to traditional kinetic armed attack in scale and effect”,"Position Paper of Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Germany/Germany_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=gvGjQ3,
Germany,DEU,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Yes,"“Assessing whether the scale and effects of the cyber operation are grave enough to consider it an armed attack is a political decision taken in the framework of international law... The decision is not made based only on technical information, but also after assessing the strategic context and the effect of the cyber operation beyond cyberspace”","Position Paper of Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Germany/Germany_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=gvGjQ3,
Germany,DEU,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,“Malicious cyber operations can constitute an armed attack whenever they are comparable to traditional kinetic armed attack in scale and effect.”,"Position Paper of Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Germany/Germany_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=gvGjQ3,
Germany,DEU,12c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Yes,"“Physical destruction of property, injury and death (including as an indirect effect) and serious territorial incursions are relevant factors.” The right to self-defence according to art. 51 UN Charter is triggered if an armed attack occurs. Malicious cyber operations can constitute an armed attack whenever they are comparable to traditional kinetic armed attack in scale and effect. Germany concurs with the view expressed in rule 71 of the Tallinn Manual 2.0.","Position Paper of Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Germany/Germany_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=gvGjQ3,
Germany,DEU,"12d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",Ambiguous,"“Physical destruction of property, injury and death (including as an indirect effect) and serious territorial incursions are relevant factors.”","Position Paper of Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Germany/Germany_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=gvGjQ3,
Germany,DEU,12e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,No,"“Germany acknowledges the view expressed in the ICJ’s Nicaragua judgment, namely that an armed attack constitutes the gravest form of use of force”","Position Paper of Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Germany/Germany_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=gvGjQ3,
Germany,DEU,12f)Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Yes,"“Germany acknowledges the view expressed in the ICJ’s Nicaragua judgment, namely that an armed attack constitutes the gravest form of use of force”","Position Paper of Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Germany/Germany_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=gvGjQ3,
Germany,DEU,12g)Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,"12h)Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,13)Can cyber operations that individually fall below the threshold of an armed attack aggregate into an armed attack triggering the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,14)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation intend to cause harms that qualify the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,15)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation produce reasonably foreseeable harms qualifying the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,16)Can cyber operations causing harms sufficient to qualify the operation as an armed attack trigger a third State’s right of self-defense if it was not the target of those operations?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,17)Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,17a)Must a State exercising collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation(s) constituting an armed attack have the victim state request or consent to such actions?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,"18)In its response to a cyber operation or operations qualifying as an armed attack, is the injured state limited to cyber means in response?",No,"“The response to malicious cyber operations constituting an armed attack is not limited to cyber counter-operations. Once the right to self-defence is triggered, the State under attack can resort to all necessary and proportionate means in order to end the attack.”","Position Paper of Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Germany/Germany_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=gvGjQ3,
Germany,DEU,19)Must a State employing the right of self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack report its actions to the UN Security Council?,Yes,"“This decision is not left to the discretion of the State victim of such a malicious cyber operation, but needs to be comprehensibly reported to the international community, i.e. the UN Security Council, according to art. 51 UN Charter”","Position Paper of Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Germany/Germany_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=gvGjQ3,
Guyana,GUY,1) Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,No.,"Consequently, a cyber operation by itself may not constitute a use of force. . .  Article 51 of the UN Charter, refers to an armed attack triggering self-defense. ln looking at the definition of cyberspace, no physical weaponry is involved and therefore a cyber operation may not be considered an armed attack, triggering self-defense by a State. A cyber operation may not qualify as use of force or an armed attack.  “",2020 OAS OAS Inter-American Juridical Committee Questionnaire - Response by Guyana,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/OAS%20Survey%20Responses/Guyana%20response.%20EN.PDF?csf=1&web=1&e=Gb4J0h,
