Ireland,IRL,11)Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,No,"28. A cyber-operation that by virtue of its scale and effects reaches the threshold of an armed attack would permit the exercise of self-defence in accordance with Article 51 and customary international law. Due to the nature of a cyber-operation, it seems that only in exceptional circumstances could it reach the threshold of “armed attack”. To reach this threshold, the scale and effects of a cyber-operation must correspond to an armed attack involving a physical use of force. It is conceivable that this need not necessitate physical damage, where for example loss or impairment of functionality to ICT infrastructure is inflicted on such a scale and with such effects that it is comparable to a conventional armed attack.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Ireland/Ireland%20(2023)%20National-Position-Paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=NgmrTg,
Ireland,IRL,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,28. A cyber-operation that by virtue of its scale and effects reaches the threshold of an armed attack would permit the exercise of self-defence.,Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Ireland/Ireland%20(2023)%20National-Position-Paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=NgmrTg,
Ireland,IRL,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,"28. ... To reach this threshold, the scale and effects of a cyber-operation must correspond to an armed attack involving a physical use of force. It is conceivable that this need not necessitate physical damage, where for example loss or impairment of functionality to ICT infrastructure is inflicted on such a scale and with such effects that it is comparable to a conventional armed attack.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Ireland/Ireland%20(2023)%20National-Position-Paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=NgmrTg,
Ireland,IRL,12c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Yes,27. ... Not every threat or use of force within the meaning of Article 2(4) of the Charter will amount to an armed attack and it is necessary to consider scale and effects. 28. A cyber-operation that by virtue of its scale and effects reaches the threshold of an armed attack would permit the exercise of self-defence in accordance with Article 51 and customary international law.,Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Ireland/Ireland%20(2023)%20National-Position-Paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=NgmrTg,
Ireland,IRL,"12d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",No,"27. ... Not every threat or use of force within the meaning of Article 2(4) of the Charter will amount to an armed attack and it is necessary to consider scale and effects. 28. A cyber-operation that by virtue of its scale and effects reaches the threshold of an armed attack would permit the exercise of self-defence in accordance with Article 51 and customary international law. Due to the nature of a cyber-operation, it seems that only in exceptional circumstances could it reach the threshold of “armed attack”. To reach this threshold, the scale and effects of a cyber-operation must correspond to an armed attack involving a physical use of force. It is conceivable that this need not necessitate physical damage, where for example loss or impairment of functionality to ICT infrastructure is inflicted on such a scale and with such effects that it is comparable to a conventional armed attack.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Ireland/Ireland%20(2023)%20National-Position-Paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=NgmrTg,
