Ireland,IRL,17)Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Yes.,"The customary international law right to self-defence is acknowledged in Article 51 of the  UN Charter, which states: “Nothing in the present Charter shall impair the inherent right of individual or collective self-defence if an armed attack occurs against a Member of the  United Nations, until the Security Council has taken measures necessary to maintain  international peace and security.” States can invoke the right to self-defence in response  to an “armed attack”.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Ireland/Ireland%20(2023)%20National-Position-Paper.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=NgmrTg,
