Italy,ITA,1)Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,There is no established definition or threshold of hostile cyber operations falling within ‘armed attack’ in the sense of article 51 of the UN Charter . Such assessment will be determined on a case-by- case basis depending on the consequences of any given cyber operation.,Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Italy/Italy_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=uLarpe,
Italy,ITA,2)Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Silent,,Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Italy/Italy_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=uLarpe,
Italy,ITA,2a)Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter,Silent,,Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Italy/Italy_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=uLarpe,
Italy,ITA,3)Does a State’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,Yes,Italy deems  that  wrongful cyber operations  conducted by State or non-State actors may constitute an armed attack when their scale and effects are comparable to those resulting from conventional armed attacks...,Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Italy/Italy_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=uLarpe,
Italy,ITA,4)Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,No,Italy deems  that  wrongful cyber operations  conducted by State or non-State actors may constitute an armed attack when their scale and effects are comparable to those resulting from conventional armed attacks...,Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Italy/Italy_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=Kgan9V,
Italy,ITA,4a)Can a State  exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,,,,
Italy,ITA,5)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Yes,"The occurrence of an armed attack triggers the right to self-defence, and the victim-State may resort to all necessary and proportionate means to end the aggression.",Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Italy/Italy_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=uLarpe,
