Italy,ITA,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,Italy deems  that  wrongful cyber operations  conducted by State or non-State actors may constitute an armed attack when their scale and effects are comparable to those resulting from conventional armed attacks...,Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Italy/Italy_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=uLarpe,
Italy,ITA,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Yes,Italy considers that the gravest form of use of force constitutes an armed attack . There is no established definition or threshold of hostile cyber operations falling within ‘armed attack’ in the sense of article 51 of the UN Charter. Such assessment will be determined on a case-by-case basis depending on the consequences of any given cyber operation.,Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Italy/Italy_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=uLarpe,
Italy,ITA,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,"Italy considers that the gravest form of use of force constitutes an armed attack . There is no established definition or threshold of hostile cyber operations falling within ‘armed attack’ in the sense of article 51 of the UN Charter . Such assessment will be determined on a case-by-case basis depending on the consequences of any given cyber operation. Italy deems  that  wrongful cyber operations  conducted by State or non-State actors may constitute an armed attack when their scale and effects are comparable to those resulting from conventional armed attacks, resulting in significant physical damage of property, human injury and loss of life, or disruption in the functioning of critical infrastructure.",Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Italy/Italy_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=uLarpe,
Italy,ITA,12c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Ambiguous,"Italy deems  that  wrongful cyber operations  conducted by State or non-State actors may constitute an armed attack when their scale and effects are comparable to those resulting from conventional armed attacks, resulting in significant physical damage of property, human injury and loss of life, or disruption in the functioning of critical infrastructure.",Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Italy/Italy_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=uLarpe,
Italy,ITA,"12d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",No,"Italy deems  that  wrongful cyber operations  conducted by State or non-State actors may constitute an armed attack when their scale and effects are comparable to those resulting from conventional armed attacks, resulting in significant physical damage of property, human injury and loss of life, or disruption in the functioning of critical infrastructure.",Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Italy/Italy_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=uLarpe,
Italy,ITA,12e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,No,"In line with the conclusions reached by the ICJ in the Nicaragua v. United States case, Italy considers that the gravest form of use of force constitutes an armed attack",Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Italy/Italy_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=uLarpe,
Italy,ITA,12f)Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Yes,"In line with the conclusions reached by the ICJ in the Nicaragua v. United States case, Italy  considers that the gravest form of use of force constitutes an armed attack .",Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Italy/Italy_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=uLarpe,
Italy,ITA,12g)Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,Yes,"Italy deems  that  wrongful cyber operations  conducted by State or non-State actors may constitute an armed attack when their scale and effects are comparable to those resulting from conventional armed attacks, resulting in significant physical damage of property, human injury and loss of life, or disruption in the functioning of critical infrastructure.",Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Italy/Italy_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=uLarpe,
Italy,ITA,"12h)Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Italy/Italy_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=uLarpe,
