Italy,ITA,15)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation produce reasonably foreseeable harms qualifying the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Italy/Italy_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=uLarpe,
Italy,ITA,16)Can cyber operations causing harms sufficient to qualify the operation as an armed attack trigger a third State’s right of self-defense if it was not the target of those operations?,Silent,,,,
Italy,ITA,17)Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Yes,"The occurrence of an armed attack triggers the right to self-defence, and the victim-State may resort to all necessary and proportionate means to end the aggression. The decision as to when a cyber operation amounting to armed attack would lead to collective self-defence will be taken on a case-by-case basis.",Italian Position Paper on International Law and Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Italy/Italy_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=uLarpe,
Italy,ITA,17a)Must a State exercising collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation(s) constituting an armed attack have the victim state request or consent to such actions?,Silent,,,,
Italy,ITA,"18)In its response to a cyber operation or operations qualifying as an armed attack, is the injured state limited to cyber means in response?",No,"The occurrence of an armed attack triggers the right to self-defence, and the victim-State may resort to all necessary and proportionate means to end the aggression.",Italy_International Law and Cyberspace.pdf,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Italy/Italy_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=uLarpe,
Italy,ITA,19)Must a State employing the right of self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack report its actions to the UN Security Council?,Silent,,,,
Japan,JPN,1)Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,"When a cyber operation constitutes an armed attack under Article 51 of the UN Charter, States may exercise the inherent right of individual or collective self-defense recognized under Article 51 of the UN Charter.",Basic Position of the Government of Japan on International Law Applicable to Cyber Operations,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Japan/Japan_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=ba8Tn2,
