Korea,KOR,1)Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,"The Republic of Korea is of the view that where the consequences of a certain cyber activity, in terms of its scale and effects, amount to an armed attack, the injured State may lawfully exercise its right of self-defense. The exercise of this right must adhere to the principles of necessity and proportionality, as required under international law Under international law, when an armed attack occurs, the injured State has the right to exercise self-defense in accordance with Article 51 of the UN Charter and relevant rules of customary international law","National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 5, §3.4 (2025)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Korea/Republic%20of%20Korea%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=hMiuk6,
Korea,KOR,2)Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Yes,"Under international law, when an armed attack occurs, the injured State has the right to exercise self-defense in accordance with Article 51 of the UN Charter and relevant rules of customary international law","National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 5, §3.4 (2025)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Korea/Republic%20of%20Korea%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=hMiuk6,
Korea,KOR,2a) Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter,Yes,"Under international law, when an armed attack occurs, the injured State has the right to exercise self-defense in accordance with Article 51 of the UN Charter and relevant rules of customary international law.","National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 5, §3.4 (2025)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Korea/Republic%20of%20Korea%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=hMiuk6,
Korea,KOR,3)Does a State’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,Silent,,,,
Korea,KOR,4)Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,Silent,,,,
Korea,KOR,4a)Can a State  exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,,,,
Korea,KOR,5)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Yes,"The Republic of Korea is of the view that where the consequences of a certain cyber activity, in terms of its scale and effects, amount to an armed attack, the injured State may lawfully exercise its right of self-defense. The exercise of this right must adhere to the principles of necessity and proportionality, as required under international law","National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 5, §3.4 (2025",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Korea/Republic%20of%20Korea%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=hMiuk6,
Korea,KOR,5a)Must force be the only option available to successfully repel an armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Korea,KOR,6)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Yes,"The Republic of Korea is of the view that where the consequences of a certain cyber activity, in terms of its scale and effects, amount to an armed attack, the injured State may lawfully exercise its right of self-defense. The exercise of this right must adhere to the principles of necessity and proportionality, as required under international law","National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 5, §3.4 (2025)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Korea/Republic%20of%20Korea%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=hMiuk6,
