Korea,KOR,10)Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Korea,KOR,11)Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
Korea,KOR,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"The Republic of Korea is of the view that where the consequences of a certain cyber activity, in terms of its scale and effects, amount to an armed attack, the injured State may lawfully exercise its right of self-defense","National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 5, §3.4 (2025)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Korea/Republic%20of%20Korea%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=hMiuk6,
Korea,KOR,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Yes,"Whether a cyber activity constitutes a ‘use of force’ must be assessed based on a holistic consideration of its consequences, nature, the identity of the actor, and the target","National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3.2 (2025)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Korea/Republic%20of%20Korea%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=hMiuk6,
Korea,KOR,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,"If a certain cyber activity results in physical effects that are equivalent to those caused by a traditional use of force under international law, it may, prima facie, be regarded as a use of force","National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3.2 (2025)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Korea/Republic%20of%20Korea%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=hMiuk6,
Korea,KOR,12c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Yes,...only those uses of force that reach a certain threshold of gravity in terms of scale and effects may be considered armed attacks,"National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3.2 (2025)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Korea/Republic%20of%20Korea%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=hMiuk6,
Korea,KOR,"12d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
Korea,KOR,12e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,No,The concept of ‘use of force’ encompasses the narrower category of ‘armed attack.’ The International Court of Justice (ICJ) has clarified that not every use of force qualifies as an armed attack; only those uses of force that reach a certain threshold of gravity in terms of scale and effects may be considered armed attacks,"National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3.2 (2025)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Korea/Republic%20of%20Korea%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=hMiuk6,
Korea,KOR,12f)Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Yes,..only those uses of force that reach a certain threshold of gravity in terms of scale and effects may be considered armed attacks,"National Position of the Republic of Korea on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3.2 (2025)",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Korea/Republic%20of%20Korea%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=hMiuk6,
