Netherlands,NLD,1) Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,A state targeted by a cyber operation that can be qualified as an armed attack may invoke its inherent right of self-defence and use force to defend itself. This right is laid down in article 51 of the UN Charter.,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Netherlands,NLD,2) Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Silent,,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Netherlands,NLD,2a) Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Silent,,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Netherlands,NLD,3) Does a State’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,Yes,States may therefore use force in self-defence only if the origin of the attack and the identity of those responsible are sufficiently certain. This applies to both state and non-state actors.,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Netherlands,NLD,4) Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,No,The burden of proof for justifiable self-defence against an armed attack is a heavy one. The government shares the conclusion of the CAVV and the AIV that ‘No form of self-defence whatever may be exercised without adequate proof of the origin or source of the attack and without convincing proof that a particular state or states or organised group is responsible for conducting or controlling the attack.’ States may therefore use force in self-defence only if the origin of the attack and the identity of those responsible are sufficiently certain. This applies to both state and non-state actors.,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Netherlands,NLD,4a) Can a State exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,5) Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Yes,"When exercising their right of self-defence, states must also meet the conditions of necessity and proportionality. In this regard the government shares the view of the CAVV and the AIV that invoking the right of self-defence is justifiable only ‘provided the intention is to end the attack, the measures do not exceed that objective and there are no viable alternatives.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Netherlands,NLD,5a) Must force be the only option available to successfully repel an armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,6) Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Yes,"When exercising their right of self-defence, states must also meet the conditions of necessity and proportionality. In this regard the government shares the view of the CAVV and the AIV that invoking the right of self-defence is justifiable only ‘provided the intention is to end the attack, the measures do not exceed that objective and there are no viable alternatives. The proportionality requirement rules out measures that harbour the risk of escalation and that are not strictly necessary to end the attack or prevent attacks in the near future.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Netherlands,NLD,7) Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,8) Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,9) Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,Ambiguous,"When exercising their right of self -defence, states must also meet the conditions of necessity and proportionality...The proportionality requirement rules out measures that harbour the risk of escalation and that are not strictly necessary to end the attack or prevent attacks in the near future.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Netherlands,NLD,10) Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,Silent,,.,,
Netherlands,NLD,11)Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,No,"When applying this prohibition in the context of cyberspace, the question arises:  when can cyber operations be considered ‘use of force’, given that no use is made of ‘weapons’ in the usual (physical) sense of the word? The government believes that  cyber operations can fall within the scope of the prohibition of the use of force, particularly when the effects of the operation are comparable to those of a conventional  act of violence covered by the prohibition. In other words, the effects of the operation  determine whether the prohibition applies, not the manner in which those effects are achieved. This position is supported by the case law of the International Court of  Justice, which has ruled that the scale and effects of an operation must be considered  when assessing whether an armed attack in the context of the right of self-defence has  taken place (see below).  International law is ambiguous on the precise scale and effects an operation must have in order to qualify as an armed attack. It is clear, however, that an armed attack does not necessarily have to be carried out by kinetic means. This view is in line with the Nuclear Weapons Advisory Opinion of the International Court of Justice, in which the Court concluded that the means by which an attack is carried out is not the decisive factor in determining whether it constitutes an armed attack.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Netherlands,NLD,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"... a cyber attack that has comparable consequences to an armed attack (fatalities, damage and destruction) can justify a response with cyber weapons or conventional weapons (...)’. There is therefore no reason not to qualify a cyberattack against a computer or information system as an armed attack if the consequences are comparable to those of an attack with conventional or non- conventional weapons.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Netherlands,NLD,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Silent,.,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Netherlands,NLD,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,"... a cyber attack that has comparable consequences to an armed attack (fatalities, damage and destruction) can justify a response with cyber weapons or conventional weapons (...)’.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Netherlands,NLD,12c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Yes,"To determine whether an operation constitutes an armed attack, the scale and effects of the operation must be considered.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Netherlands,NLD,"12d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",Ambiguous,"There is therefore no reason not to qualify a cyberattack against a computer or information system as an armed attack if the consequences are comparable to those of an attack with conventional or non- conventional weapons. At present there is no international consensus on qualifying a cyberattack as an  armed attack if it does not cause fatalities, physical damage or destruction yet  nevertheless has very serious non-material consequences",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Netherlands,NLD,12e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,No,An armed attack is not the same as the use of force within the meaning of article 2(4) of the UN Charter.,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Netherlands,NLD,12f)Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Yes,"In the Nicaragua case, the International Court of Justice defined an armed attack as the most serious form of the use of force.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Netherlands,NLD,12g)Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,Ambiguous,"In their 2011 advisory report ‘Cyber Warfare’, the Advisory Council on International Affairs (AIV) and the Advisory Committee on Issues of Public International Law (CAVV) noted that, ‘The customary interpretation of this provision is that all forms of armed force are  prohibited. Purely economic, diplomatic and political pressure or coercion is not  defined as force under article 2, paragraph 4. Suspending trade relations or freezing assets, for example, can be very disadvantageous to the state affected but has not to date been considered a prohibited form of forcewithin the meaning of the Charter. Armed force that has a real or potential physical impact on the target state is prohibited.’ In the view of the government, at this time it cannot be ruled out that  a cyber operation with a very serious financial or economic impact may qualify as the use of force.  There is therefore no reason not to qualify a cyberattack against a computer or information system as an armed attack if the consequences are comparable to those of an attack with conventional or non- conventional weapons.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Netherlands,NLD,"12h)Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",Ambiguous,"In their 2011 advisory report ‘Cyber Warfare’, the Advisory Council on International Affairs (AIV) and the Advisory Committee on Issues of Public International Law (CAVV) noted that, ‘The customary interpretation of this provision is that all forms of armed force are  prohibited. Purely economic, diplomatic and political pressure or coercion is not  defined as force under article 2, paragraph 4. Suspending trade relations or freezing assets, for example, can be very disadvantageous to the state affected but has not to date been considered a prohibited form of forcewithin the meaning of the Charter. Armed force that has a real or potential physical impact on the target state is prohibited.’ In the view of the government, at this time it cannot be ruled out that  a cyber operation with a very serious financial or economic impact may qualify as the use of force.  At present there is no international consensus on qualifying a cyberattack as an armed attack if it does not cause fatalities, physical damage or destruction yet nevertheless has very serious non-material consequences",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Netherlands,NLD,13)Can cyber operations that individually fall below the threshold of an armed attack aggregate into an armed attack triggering the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,14)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation intend to cause harms that qualify the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,15)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation produce reasonably foreseeable harms qualifying the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,16)Can cyber operations causing harms sufficient to qualify the operation as an armed attack trigger a third State’s right of self-defense if it was not the target of those operations?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,17)Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,17a)Must a State exercising collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation(s) constituting an armed attack have the victim state request or consent to such actions?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,"18)In its response to a cyber operation or operations qualifying as an armed attack, is the injured state limited to cyber means in response?",No,"... ‘a cyber attack that has comparable consequences to an armed attack (fatalities, damage and destruction) can justify a response with cyber weapons or conventional weapons (...)’.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Netherlands,NLD,19)Must a State employing the right of self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack report its actions to the UN Security Council?,Silent,,,,
New Zealand,NZL,1) Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,24. A state subjected to malicious cyber activity amounting to an armed attack has further recourse to the inherent right of individual and/or collective self-defence in accordance with Article 51 of the UN Charter.,"New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/New%20Zealand/New%20Zealand%20Statement%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=iczbyp,
New Zealand,NZL,2) Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Yes,24 b. Any exercise of that right: ...must be consistent with relevant UN Charter and customary international law obligations...,"New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/New%20Zealand/New%20Zealand%20Statement%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=iczbyp,
New Zealand,NZL,2a) Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Yes,24. A state subjected to malicious cyber activity amounting to an armed attack has further recourse to the inherent right of individual and/or collective self-defence in accordance with Article 51 of the UN Charter... Any exercise of that right:  b. must be consistent with relevant UN Charter and customary international law obligations...,"New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/New%20Zealand/New%20Zealand%20Statement%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=iczbyp,
New Zealand,NZL,3) Does a State’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,Silent,,,,
New Zealand,NZL,4) Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,Silent,,"New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/New%20Zealand/New%20Zealand%20Statement%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=iczbyp,
New Zealand,NZL,4a) Can a State exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,,,,
New Zealand,NZL,5) Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Yes,"24. A state subjected to malicious cyber activity amounting to an armed attack has further recourse to the inherent right of individual and/or collective self-defence in accordance with Article 51 of the UN Charter... Any exercise of that right:  b. must be consistent with relevant UN Charter and customary international law obligations, including notification to the United Nations, necessity, and proportionality.","New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/New%20Zealand/New%20Zealand%20Statement%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=iczbyp,
New Zealand,NZL,5a) Must force be the only option available to successfully repel an armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Silent,,"New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/New%20Zealand/New%20Zealand%20Statement%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=iczbyp,
New Zealand,NZL,6) Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Yes,"24. A state subjected to malicious cyber activity amounting to an armed attack has further recourse to the inherent right of individual and/or collective self-defence in accordance with Article 51 of the UN Charter... Any exercise of that right:  b. must be consistent with relevant UN Charter and customary international law obligations, including notification to the United Nations, necessity, and proportionality.","New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/New%20Zealand/New%20Zealand%20Statement%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=iczbyp,
New Zealand,NZL,7) Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Yes,"24. A state subjected to malicious cyber activity amounting to an armed attack has further recourse to the inherent right of individual and/or collective self-defence in accordance with Article 51 of the UN Charter. The right to self-defence also arises when an armed attack is imminent, including by cyber means.","New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/New%20Zealand/New%20Zealand%20Statement%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=iczbyp,
New Zealand,NZL,8) Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Silent,,,,
New Zealand,NZL,9) Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,Yes,"24. A state subjected to malicious cyber activity amounting to an armed attack has further recourse to the inherent right of individual and/or collective self-defence in accordance with Article 51 of the UN Charter. The right to self-defence also arises when an armed attack is imminent, including by cyber means.","New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/New%20Zealand/New%20Zealand%20Statement%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=iczbyp,
