New Zealand,NZL,13)Can cyber operations that individually fall below the threshold of an armed attack aggregate into an armed attack triggering the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
New Zealand,NZL,14)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation intend to cause harms that qualify the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Ambiguous,"In assessing the scale and effects of malicious state cyber activity, states may take into account both the immediate impacts and the intended or reasonably expected consequential impacts.","New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/New%20Zealand/New%20Zealand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=iE0MfM,
New Zealand,NZL,15)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation produce reasonably foreseeable harms qualifying the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Ambiguous,"In assessing the scale and effects of malicious state cyber activity, states may take into account both the immediate impacts and the intended or reasonably expected consequential impacts.","New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/New%20Zealand/New%20Zealand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=iE0MfM,
New Zealand,NZL,16)Can cyber operations causing harms sufficient to qualify the operation as an armed attack trigger a third State’s right of self-defense if it was not the target of those operations?,Silent,,,,
New Zealand,NZL,17)Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Yes,26. A state subjected to malicious cyber activity amounting to an armed attack has further recourse to the inherent right of individual and/or collective self-defence in accordance with Article 51 of the UN Charter.,"New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/New%20Zealand/New%20Zealand%20Statement%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=iczbyp,
New Zealand,NZL,17a)Must a State exercising collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation(s) constituting an armed attack have the victim state request or consent to such actions?,Silent,,,,
New Zealand,NZL,"18)In its response to a cyber operation or operations qualifying as an armed attack, is the injured state limited to cyber means in response?",No,"Any exercise of that right: a. may include, but is not limited to, cyber activities...","New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/New%20Zealand/New%20Zealand%20Statement%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=iczbyp,
New Zealand,NZL,19)Must a State employing the right of self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack report its actions to the UN Security Council?,Yes,"... must be consistent with relevant UN Charter and customary international law obligations, including notification to the United Nations, necessity, and proportionality","New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace New Zealand, The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/New%20Zealand/New%20Zealand%20Statement%20(2020).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=iczbyp,
Norway,NOR,1) Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,"5.4 A State that is the victim of a cyber operation that qualifies as an armed attack under international law, may exercise its inherent right of individual or collective self-defence under Article 51 of the UN Charter.","Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Norway/Norway%20(2021).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=thG62a,
Norway,NOR,2) Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Yes,"5.4 The right of self-defence as reflected in Article 51 is a norm of customary international law. It must be exercised subject to the requirements of necessity and proportionality, and may involve both digital and conventional means.","Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Norway/Norway%20(2021).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=thG62a,
Norway,NOR,2a) Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Ambiguous,"5.4 A State that is the victim of a cyber operation that qualifies as an armed attack under international law, may exercise its inherent right of individual or collective self-defence under Article 51 of the UN Charter","Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Norway/Norway%20(2021).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=thG62a,
Norway,NOR,3) Does a State’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,Silent,,,,
Norway,NOR,4) Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,Silent,,"Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Norway/Norway%20(2021).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=thG62a,
Norway,NOR,4a) Can a State exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,,,,
Norway,NOR,5) Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Yes,"5.4 The right of self-defence as reflected in Article 51 is a norm of customary international law. It must be exercised subject to the requirements of necessity and proportionality, and may involve both digital and conventional means.","Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace, , pg. 9, ¶5.4 (2021).",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Norway/Norway%20(2021).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=thG62a,
Norway,NOR,5a) Must force be the only option available to successfully repel an armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Silent,,"Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Norway/Norway%20(2021).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=thG62a,
Norway,NOR,6) Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Yes,"5.4 The right of self-defence as reflected in Article 51 is a norm of customary international law. It must be exercised subject to the requirements of necessity and proportionality, and may involve both digital and conventional means.","Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Norway/Norway%20(2021).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=thG62a,
Norway,NOR,7) Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Silent,,"Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Norway/Norway%20(2021).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=thG62a,
Norway,NOR,8) Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Silent,,"Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Norway/Norway%20(2021).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=thG62a,
Norway,NOR,9) Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Norway,NOR,10) Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Norway,NOR,11)Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
Norway,NOR,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"A State that is the victim of a cyber operation that qualifies as an armed attack under international law, may exercise its inherent right of individual or collective self-defence under Article 51 of the UN Charter.","Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Norway/Norway%20(2021).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=thG62a,
Norway,NOR,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Silent,,,,
Norway,NOR,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,cyber operation may constitute use of force or even an armed attack if its scale and effects are comparable to those of the use of force or an armed attack by conventional means.,,,
Norway,NOR,12c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Silent,,,,
Norway,NOR,"12d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",No,"Cyber operations that cause death or injury to persons or physical damage to or the destruction of objects could clearly amount to the use of force. Likewise, a cyber operation causing severe disruption to the  functioning of the State such as the use of crypto viruses or other forms of digital sabotage against governmental or private power grid- or telecommunications infrastructure, or cyber operations leading to  the destruction of stockpiles of Covid-19 vaccines, could amount to the use of force in violation of Article 2(4). Similarly, the use of crypto viruses or other forms of digital sabotage against a State’s financial and  banking system, or other operations that cause widespread economic effects and destabilisation, may amount to the use of force in violation of Article 2(4). A cyber operation that severely damages or disables a State’s critical infrastructure or functions may furthermore be considered as amounting to an armed attack under international law. Depending on its scale  and effect, this may include a cyber operation that causes an aircraft crash.","Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Norway/Norway%20(2021).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=thG62a,
Norway,NOR,12e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,No,"In accordance with the case law of the  International Court of Justice (ICJ), an armed attack is the gravest form of the use of force","Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Norway/Norway%20(2021).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=thG62a,
Norway,NOR,12f) Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Yes,"In accordance with the case law of the  International Court of Justice (ICJ), an armed attack is the gravest form of the use of force","Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Norway/Norway%20(2021).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=thG62a,
Norway,NOR,12g)Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,Yes,"Cyber operations that cause death or injury to persons or physical damage to or the destruction of objects could clearly amount to the use of force. Likewise, a cyber operation causing severe disruption to the  functioning of the State such as the use of crypto viruses or other forms of digital sabotage against governmental or private power grid- or telecommunications infrastructure, or cyber operations leading to  the destruction of stockpiles of Covid-19 vaccines, could amount to the use of force in violation of Article 2(4). Similarly, the use of crypto viruses or other forms of digital sabotage against a State’s financial and  banking system, or other operations that cause widespread economic effects and destabilisation, may amount to the use of force in violation of Article 2(4). A cyber operation that severely damages or disables a State’s critical infrastructure or functions mayfurthermore be considered as amounting to an armed attack under international law. Depending on its scale  and effect, this may include a cyber operation that causes an aircraft crash.","Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Norway/Norway%20(2021).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=thG62a,
Norway,NOR,"12h)Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
