Norway,NOR,17)Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Yes,"A State that is the victim of a cyber operation that qualifies as an armed attack under international law, may exercise its inherent right of individual or collective self-defence under Article 51 of the UN Charter.","Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Norway/Norway%20(2021).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=thG62a,
Norway,NOR,17a)Must a State exercising collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation(s) constituting an armed attack have the victim state request or consent to such actions?,Silent,,,,
Norway,NOR,"18)In its response to a cyber operation or operations qualifying as an armed attack, is the injured state limited to cyber means in response?",No,"The right of self-defence as reflected in Article 51 is a norm of customary international law. It must be exercised subject to the requirements of necessity and proportionality, and may involve both digital and conventional means.","Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace,",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Norway/Norway%20(2021).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=thG62a,
