Peru,PER,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"Based on the foregoing, one might reasonably say that a cyber operation that entails or causes damage of that order of magnitude to military targets or individuals or property protected by international humanitarian law could in itself constitute a use of force. In that sense, the inherent right of states to legitimate self-defense under Article 51 of the United Nations Charter ought to be considered valid.",OAS Survey Response Submitted by Peru,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/OAS%20Survey%20Responses/Response%20by%20Peru.%20EN.def.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=3OkBc2,
Peru,PER,12)a. Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Silent,,,,
Peru,PER,12)b. Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,"Based on the foregoing, one might reasonably say that a cyber operation that entails or causes damage of that order of magnitude to military targets or individuals or property protected by international humanitarian law could in itself constitute a use of force. In that sense, the inherent right of states to legitimate self-defense under Article 51 of the United Nations Charter ought to be considered valid.",OAS Survey Response Submitted by Peru,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/OAS%20Survey%20Responses/Response%20by%20Peru.%20EN.def.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=3OkBc2,
Peru,PER,12)c. Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Ambiguous,"With respect to the possibility of a cyber operation that does not cause violent effects being classed as a use of force or an armed attack, it should be noted that if military damage is not caused, the threshold would be met if the act was likely to cause death or injury to persons or property protected from attacks, provided that the military act also satisfies the requirements of direct causation and belligerent nexus.",OAS Survey Response Submitted by Peru,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/OAS%20Survey%20Responses/Response%20by%20Peru.%20EN.def.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=3OkBc2,
Peru,PER,"12)d. Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",No,"Just as Article 2.4 is applicable to cyberspace, a State the sustains an armed attack can invoke the right of legitimate self-defense under Article 51 of the Charter. As to whether a cyber operation can qualify as a use of force or an armed attack without causing violent effects, this General Office is of the opinion that a use of force does not necessarily cause violent effects such that it should be regarded as violating Article 2.4 of the Charter. An interesting scenario in that regard is when a state fires a missile through the airspace of a neighboring state without it descending into its territorial space. Such an act does not produce material damage but it does violate the territory of the neighboring state; accordingly, although it has not generated “violent effects,” it infringes the principle contained in the aforementioned Article 2.4 nevertheless.    With respect to the possibility of a cyber operation that does not cause violent effects being classed as a use of force or an armed attack, it should be noted that if military damage is not caused, the threshold would be met if the act was likely to cause death or injury to persons or property protected from attacks, provided that the military act also satisfies the requirements of direct causation and belligerent nexus.",OAS Survey Response Submitted by Peru,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/OAS%20Survey%20Responses/Response%20by%20Peru.%20EN.def.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=3OkBc2,
Peru,PER,12)e. Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,Silent .,,OAS Survey Response Submitted by Peru,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/OAS%20Survey%20Responses/Response%20by%20Peru.%20EN.def.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=3OkBc2,
Peru,PER,12)f. Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Yes,"As the International Court of Justice has held, it will be necessary to distinguish the most grave forms of the use of force (those constituting an armed attack) from other less grave forms... Accordingly, a cyber operation could give rise to a less grave use of force and, therefore, breach Article 2.4 of the Charter.  Just as Article 2.4 is applicable to cyberspace, a State the [sic] sustains an armed attack can invoke the right of legitimate self-defense under Article 51 of the Charter.",OAS Survey Response Submitted by Peru,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/OAS%20Survey%20Responses/Response%20by%20Peru.%20EN.def.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=3OkBc2,
Peru,PER,12)g. Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,"Ambiguous,","Cyber operations can exceed the threshold of mere unfriendly activities on the web and qualify as a use of force if people and public or private property are physically harmed as a result of a cyber attack. Context is crucial in making an evaluation in that regard, as is identifying the actors perpretrating the attack, the target, the effects and the intent. Clear examples would be a cyber attack on a nuclear power plant or the control, manipulation, neutralization, and destruction of an enemy arsenal by means of virtual networks.  Peru more openly admitted the “possibility of a cyber operation that does not cause violent effects being classed as a use of force or an armed attack.”",OAS Survey Response Submitted by Peru,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/OAS%20Survey%20Responses/Response%20by%20Peru.%20EN.def.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=3OkBc2,
Peru,PER,"12)h. Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
Peru,PER,13. Can cyber operations that individually fall below the threshold of an armed attack aggregate into an armed attack triggering the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Peru,PER,14. Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation intend to cause harms that qualify the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Ambiguou.,"Context is crucial in making an evaluation in that regard, as is identifying the actors perpetrating the attack, the target, the effects and the intent.",OAS Survey Response Submitted by Peru,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/OAS%20Survey%20Responses/Response%20by%20Peru.%20EN.def.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=3OkBc2,
Peru,PER,15. Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation produce reasonably foreseeable harms qualifying the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,No  Revert to silent,"With respect to the possibility of a cyber operation that does not cause violent effects being classed as a use of force or an armed attack, it should be noted that if military damage is not caused, the threshold would be met if the act was likely to cause death or injury to persons or property protected from attacks, provided that the military act also satisfies the requirements of direct causation and belligerent nexus.",,,
Peru,PER,16. Can cyber operations causing harms sufficient to qualify the operation as an armed attack trigger a third State’s right of self-defense if it was not the target of those operations?,Silent,,,,
Peru,PER,17. Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Silent,,,,
Peru,PER,17)a. Must a State exercising collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation(s) constituting an armed attack have the victim state request or consent to such actions?,Silent,,,,
Peru,PER,"18. In its response to a cyber operation or operations qualifying as an armed attack, is the injured state limited to cyber means in response?",Silent,,,,
Peru,PER,19. Must a State employing the right of self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack report its actions to the UN Security Council?,Silent,,,,
Poland,POL,1) Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,"A cyberattack may be qualified as an armed attack. The right to self-defence applies to cyberspace. Pursuant to Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations and customary international law, a state has the right of self-defence in the event of an armed attack. In the context of cyberspace, a cyberattack that results in death or injury of people or damage or destruction of property of significant value may be considered an armed attack. In such circumstances, according to international law, a state enjoys the right of self-defence, however, this right should be exercised in line with the principles arising from customary international law, namely the principle of necessity and proportionality.","Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg.",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Poland/Poland’s_position%20(2022).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=agMh8n,
Poland,POL,2) Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Yes,"Pursuant to Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations and customary international law, a state has the right of self-defence in the event of an armed attack.","Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg.",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Poland/Poland’s_position%20(2022).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=agMh8n,
Poland,POL,2a) Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Yes.,"Pursuant to Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations and customary international law, a state has the right of self-defence in the event of an armed attack.","Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg.",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Poland/Poland’s_position%20(2022).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=agMh8n,
Poland,POL,3) Does a State’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,Yes,"According to international law, the right of self-defence may also apply to cyberattacks reaching the threshold of an armed attack inflicted by non-state actors. The right of collective self-defence applies to cyberspace as well.","Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg.",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Poland/Poland’s_position%20(2022).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=agMh8n,
Poland,POL,4) Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,No,"According to international law, the right of self-defence may also apply to cyberattacks reaching the threshold of an armed attack inflicted by non-state actors. The right of collective self-defence applies to cyberspace as well.","Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg.",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Poland/Poland’s_position%20(2022).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=agMh8n,
Poland,POL,4a) Can a State exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,,,,
Poland,POL,5) Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Yes,"Pursuant to Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations and customary international law, a state has the right of self-defence in the event of an armed attack. In the context of cyberspace, a cyberattack that results in death or injury of people or damage or destruction of property of significant value may be considered an armed attack. In such circumstances, according to international law, a state enjoys the right of self-defence, however, this right should be exercised in line with the principles arising from customary international law, namely the principle of necessity and proportionality.","Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg.",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Poland/Poland’s_position%20(2022).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=agMh8n,
Poland,POL,5a) Must force be the only option available to successfully repel an armed attack under the right of self-defense?,SilentNo,,"Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg.",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Poland/Poland’s_position%20(2022).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=agMh8n,
Poland,POL,6) Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Yes,"Pursuant to Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations and customary international law, a state has the right of self-defence in the event of an armed attack. In the context of cyberspace, a cyberattack that results in death or injury of people or damage or destruction of property of significant value may be considered an armed attack. In such circumstances, according to international law, a state enjoys the right of self-defence, however, this right should be exercised in line with the principles arising from customary international law, namely the principle of necessity and proportionality.","Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg.",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Poland/Poland’s_position%20(2022).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=agMh8n,
