Poland,POL,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"Pursuant to Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations and customary international law, a state has the right of self-defence in the event of an armed attack. In the context of cyberspace, a cyberattack that results in the death or injury of people or damage or destruction of property of significant value may be considered an armed attack.","Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg.",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Poland/Poland’s_position%20(2022).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=agMh8n,
Poland,POL,12)a. Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Yes,"What matters are the effects of the actions taken. As a result, it cannot be ruled out that in some circumstances a cyberattack will reach such a threshold that it will be deemed the use of force.... When assessing whether or not a cyber operation reaches the threshold of the use of force, the situation must be analyzed individually, taking into consideration the circumstances of actions taken in accordance with the requirements of international law.... This list is not exhaustive – the legal qualification will always depend on the circumstances of a particular attack.  ... In the context of cyberspace, a cyberattack that results in death or injury of people or damage or destruction of property of significant value may be considered an armed attack.","Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg.",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Poland/Poland’s_position%20(2022).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=agMh8n,
Poland,POL,12)b. Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes.,What matters are the effects of the actions taken. ... [A] cyberattack that results in death or injury of people or damage or destruction of property of significant value may be considered an armed attack.,"Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg.",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Poland/Poland’s_position%20(2022).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=agMh8n,
Poland,POL,12)c. Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Silent,,,,
