Singapore,SGP,1)Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,"While Singapore considers the above principles to be essential ones underpinning the international legal order, Singapore’s position is that it bears noting that ultimately, none of these impair a State’s inherent right of self-defence, as provided under the UN Charter. This right of self-defence also applies in the cyber domain. In other words, a State has the inherent right of self-defence if malicious cyber activity amounting to an armed attack, or an imminent threat thereof, occurs against that state.","Singapore, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Singapore,SGP,2)Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,2a)Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter,Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,3)Does a State’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,4)Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,4a)Can a State  exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,,,,
