Singapore,SGP,10)Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,11)Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"A State has the inherent right of self-defence if malicious cyber activity amounting to an armed attack, or an imminent threat thereof, occurs against that State.","Singapore, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Singapore,SGP,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,"In other words, a State has the inherent right of self-defence if malicious cyber activity amounting to an armed attack, or an imminent threat thereof, occurs against that State. Malicious cyber activity attributable to a State that causes death, injury, physical damage or destruction equivalent to a traditional non-cyber armed attack or presenting an imminent threat thereof, would constitute an armed attack.","Singapore, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Singapore,SGP,12c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Yes,"In Singapore’s view, it is also possible that, in certain limited circumstances, malicious cyber activity may amount to an armed attack even if it does not necessarily cause death, injury, physical damage or destruction, taking into account the scale and effects of the cyber activity.","Singapore, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Singapore,SGP,"12d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",No,"In Singapore’s view, it is also possible that, in certain limited circumstances, malicious cyber activity may amount to an armed attack even if it does not necessarily cause death, injury, physical damage or destruction, taking into account the scale and effects of the cyber activity.","Singapore, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Singapore,SGP,12e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,12f)Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,12g)Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,Yes,"In Singapore’s view, it is also possible that, in certain limited circumstances, malicious cyber activity may amount to an armed attack even if it does not necessarily cause death, injury, physical damage or destruction, taking into account the scale and effects of the cyber activity. An example might be a targeted cyber operation causing a sustained and long-term outage of Singapore’s critical infrastructure.","Singapore, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Singapore,SGP,"12h)Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",Yes,"In Singapore’s view, it is also possible that, in certain limited circumstances, malicious cyber activity may amount to an armed attack even if it does not necessarily cause death, injury, physical damage or destruction, taking into account the scale and effects of the cyber activity. An example might be a targeted cyber operation causing a sustained and long-term outage of Singapore’s critical infrastructure.","Singapore, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
Singapore,SGP,13)Can cyber operations that individually fall below the threshold of an armed attack aggregate into an armed attack triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"A series or combination of cyber-attacks, whether or not it is in combination with kinetic attacks, may amount to an armed attack, even if the individual attacks do not reach the threshold equivalent to an armed attack, as long as the attacks are launched by the same actor or by different attackers acting in concert.","Singapore, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
