Sweden,SWE,11)Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,Ambiguous,"Acts that constitute the use of force are not clearly defined in international law. The ICJ has declared that the provisions on the use of force are not dependent on the choice of means, but rather apply to any use of force regardless of the weapons employed.",Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Sweden/swedens-position-paper-on-the-application-of-international-law-in-cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=2GIU3a,
Sweden,SWE,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,An attack by cyber means may have the potential to constitute an armed attack if its scale and effects are comparable to an armed attack by kinetic means.,Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Sweden/swedens-position-paper-on-the-application-of-international-law-in-cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=2GIU3a,
Sweden,SWE,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Yes,An assessment needs to be made on a case-by-case basis.,Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Sweden/swedens-position-paper-on-the-application-of-international-law-in-cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=2GIU3a,
Sweden,SWE,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,An attack by cyber means may have the potential to constitute an armed attack if its scale and effects are comparable to an armed attack by kinetic means.,Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Sweden/swedens-position-paper-on-the-application-of-international-law-in-cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=2GIU3a,
Sweden,SWE,12c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Yes,The court has furthermore distinguished the most grave forms of the use of force (those constituting an armed attack) from other less grave forms based on scale and effects.  ... An attack by cyber means may have the potential to constitute an armed attack if its scale and effects are comparable to an armed attack by kinetic means.,Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Sweden/swedens-position-paper-on-the-application-of-international-law-in-cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=2GIU3a,
Sweden,SWE,"12d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
Sweden,SWE,12e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,No,The Court has furthmore distinguished the most grave forms of the use of force (those constituting an armed attack) from other less grave forms based on scale and effects. A similar assessment of scale and effects may be made in relation to whether an act constitutes a breach of the prohibition of the use of force.,Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Sweden/swedens-position-paper-on-the-application-of-international-law-in-cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=2GIU3a,
Sweden,SWE,12f)Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Yes,The Court has furthermore distinguished the most grave forms of the use of force (those constituting an armed attack) from other less grave forms based on scale and effects.,Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Sweden/swedens-position-paper-on-the-application-of-international-law-in-cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=2GIU3a,
Sweden,SWE,12g) Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
Sweden,SWE,"12h)Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
Sweden,SWE,13)Can cyber operations that individually fall below the threshold of an armed attack aggregate into an armed attack triggering the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Sweden,SWE,14)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation intend to cause harms that qualify the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Sweden,SWE,15)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation produce reasonably foreseeable harms qualifying the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Sweden,SWE,16)Can cyber operations causing harms sufficient to qualify the operation as an armed attack trigger a third State’s right of self-defense if it was not the target of those operations?,Silent,,,,
Sweden,SWE,17)Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Yes,"The only exceptions in the Un Charter are the inherent right of individual or collective self-defence if an armed attack occurs, or acts taken pursuant to a decision of the UN Security Council authorising the use of force.",Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Sweden/swedens-position-paper-on-the-application-of-international-law-in-cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=2GIU3a,
Sweden,SWE,17a)Must a State exercising collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation(s) constituting an armed attack have the victim state request or consent to such actions?,Silent,,,,
Sweden,SWE,"18)In its response to a cyber operation or operations qualifying as an armed attack, is the injured state limited to cyber means in response?",No,"It is not a requirement under the right of self-defence that the armed attack use kinetic means, nor that the use of force in self-defence is limited to such means.",Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Sweden/swedens-position-paper-on-the-application-of-international-law-in-cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=2GIU3a,
Sweden,SWE,19)Must a State employing the right of self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack report its actions to the UN Security Council?,Yes,The exercise of the right of self-defence needs to be reported to the Security Council.,Sweden’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Sweden/swedens-position-paper-on-the-application-of-international-law-in-cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=2GIU3a,
Switzerland,CHE,1)Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,One of the key founding principles of the UN Charter is the prohibition on the use of force (Art. 2 para. 4). There are only two exceptions: if the use of force is authorised by the UN Security Council (Art. 42) or if the strict conditions under which the right of self-defence may be exercised are fulfilled (Art. 51).  The prohibition on the use of force and the right of self-defence are also applicable to cyberspace.,Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Switzerland/Switzerland%27s-statement.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=qdmHVd,
Switzerland,CHE,2)Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,2a) Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter,Silent,,Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Switzerland/Switzerland%27s-statement.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=qdmHVd,
Switzerland,CHE,3)Does a State’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,4)Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,4a)Can a State  exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,5)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Yes,"Consequently, even if an armed attack occurs, a state is only permitted to undertake countermeasures that are necessary and proportionate in order to repel the attack.",Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Switzerland/Switzerland%27s-statement.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=qdmHVd,
Switzerland,CHE,5a)Must force be the only option available to successfully repel and armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,6)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Yes,"Consequently, even if an armed attack occurs, a state is only permitted to undertake countermeasures that are necessary and proportionate in order to repel the attack.",Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Switzerland/Switzerland%27s-statement.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=qdmHVd,
Switzerland,CHE,7)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,8)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,9)Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,No,The right of self-defence may only be exercised if an armed attack occurs first.,Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Switzerland/Switzerland%27s-statement.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=qdmHVd,
Switzerland,CHE,10)Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,No,The right of self-defence may only be exercised if an armed attack occurs first.,Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Switzerland/Switzerland%27s-statement.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=qdmHVd,
Switzerland,CHE,11)Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,No,The ICJ has also determined that an armed attack does not necessarily have to involve kinetic military action or the use of weapons because the means by which an attack is perpetrated is not the decisive factor.,Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Switzerland/Switzerland%27s-statement.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=qdmHVd,
Switzerland,CHE,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"A state is permitted to exercise its right of self-defence in response to a cyber incident if the incident amounts in scale and effect to that of a kinetic operation in terms of inflicting death or serious injury to persons, or extensive material damage to objects.",Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Switzerland/Switzerland%27s-statement.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=qdmHVd,
Switzerland,CHE,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Yes,A definitive assessment of a cyber incident in terms of international law is only possible when the concrete circumstances are known. This means interpreting and applying the rules set out below in each individual case. ... There are no binding or qualitative guidelines as to when the threshold of an armed attack in terms of scale and effect has been reached.,Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Switzerland/Switzerland%27s-statement.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=qdmHVd,
Switzerland,CHE,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,"A state is permitted to exercise its right of self-defence in response to a cyber incident if the incident amounts in scale and effect to that of a kinetic operation in terms of inflicting death or serious injury to persons, or extensive material damage to objects.",Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Switzerland/Switzerland%27s-statement.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=qdmHVd,
Switzerland,CHE,12c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,No  Move to ambiguous but note our division of views,"There are no binding or qualitative guidelines as to when the threshold of an armed attack in terms of scale and effect has been reached. A state is permitted to exercise its right of self-defence in response to a cyber incident if the incident amounts in scale and effect to that of a kinetic operation in terms of inflicting death or serious injury to persons, or extensive material damage to objects. There are no binding quantitative or qualitative guidelines as to when the threshold of an armed attack in terms of scale and effect has been reached. Current discussions on how to define an armed attack in cyberspace are focusing on attacks on critical infrastructure (e.g. nuclear power plants, power grids) which reach the required threshold in terms of scale and effect i.e. serious injury to persons and/or extensive damage to objects.",Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Switzerland/Switzerland%27s-statement.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=qdmHVd,
Switzerland,CHE,"12d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",Ambiguous,"A state is permitted to exercise its right of self-defence in response to a cyber incident if the incident amounts in scale and effect to that of a kinetic operation in terms of inflicting death or serious injury to persons, or extensive material damage to objects. There are no binding or qualitative guidelines as to when the threshold of an armed attack in terms of scale and effect has been reached. Current discussions on how to define an armed attack in cyberspace are focusing on attacks on critical infrastructure (e.g. nuclear power plants, power grids) which reach the required threshold in terms of scale and effect i.e. serious injury to persons and/or extensive damage to objects.",Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Switzerland/Switzerland%27s-statement.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=qdmHVd,
Switzerland,CHE,12e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,No,"The right of self-defence may only be exercised if an armed attack occurs first. In accordance with ICJ case law, not every violation of the prohibition on the use of force constitutes an armed attack, but only its gravest form.",Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Switzerland/Switzerland%27s-statement.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=qdmHVd,
Switzerland,CHE,12f)Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Yes,"The right of self-defence may only be exercised if an armed attack occurs first. In accordance with ICJ case law, not every violation of the prohibition on the use of force constitutes an armed attack, but only its gravest form.",Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Switzerland/Switzerland%27s-statement.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=qdmHVd,
Switzerland,CHE,12g)Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,No,"Switzerland recognises that defining what constitutes a violation of the principle of sovereignty in cyberspace is particularly challenging and has yet to be clarified conclusively. It supports considering the following two criteria in such assessments: first, does the incident violate the state's territorial integrity and second, does it constitute interference with or usurpation of an inherently governmental function. A precise definition of these criteria is a question of interpretation and subject to debate. The current debate includes among other aspects i) incidents whereby the functionality of infrastructure or related equipment has been damaged or limited, ii) cases where data has been altered or deleted, interfering with the fulfilment of inherently governmental functions such as providing social services, conducting elections and referendums, or collecting taxes, and iii) situations in which a state has sought to influence, disrupt or delay democratic decision-making processes in another state through the coordinated use of legal and illegal methods in cyberspace e.g. propaganda, disinformation and covert actions by intelligence services. The assessment of an individual case depends on the nature of the cyber incident and its repercussions.",Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Switzerland/Switzerland%27s-statement.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=qdmHVd,
Switzerland,CHE,"12h)Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,13)Can cyber operations that individually fall below the threshold of an armed attack aggregate into an armed attack triggering the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,14)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation intend to cause harms that qualify the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,15)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation produce reasonably foreseeable harms qualifying the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,16)Can cyber operations causing harms sufficient to qualify the operation as an armed attack trigger a third State’s right of self-defense if it was not the target of those operations?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,17)Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,17a)Must a State exercising collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation(s) constituting an armed attack have the victim state request or consent to such actions?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,"18)In its response to a cyber operation or operations qualifying as an armed attack, is the injured state limited to cyber means in response?",Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,19)Must a State employing the right of self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack report its actions to the UN Security Council?,Ambiguous,The right of self-defence only applies if the UN Security Council has not taken the necessary measures to maintain international peace and security (Art. 51 UN Charter).,Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Switzerland/Switzerland%27s-statement.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=qdmHVd,
Thailand,THA,1)Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,24. Thailand affirms that the inherent right of self-defence under international law including Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations applies fully in cyberspace.,Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=Ndqd7q,
Thailand,THA,2)Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Yes,24. Thailand affirms that the inherent right of self-defence under international law including Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations applies fully in cyberspace.,Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=Ndqd7q,
Thailand,THA,2a)Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter,Ambiguous,24. Thailand affirms that the inherent right of self-defence under international law including Article 51 of the Charter of the United Nations applies fully in cyberspace.,Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=Ndqd7q,
Thailand,THA,3)Does a State’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,4)Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,4a)Can a State  exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,5)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Yes,"26. ... The invocation of self-defence must strictly comply with the principles of necessity, proportionality, and immediacy, and must be based on responsible and reasonably certain attribution to a State.",Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=Ndqd7q,
Thailand,THA,5a)Must force be the only option available to successfully repel and armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,6)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Yes,"26. ... The invocation of self-defence must strictly comply with the principles of necessity, proportionality, and immediacy, and must be based on responsible and reasonably certain attribution to a State.",Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=Ndqd7q,
Thailand,THA,7)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,8)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Yes,"26. ... The invocation of self-defence must strictly comply with the principles of necessity, proportionality, and immediacy, and must be based on responsible and reasonably certain attribution to a State.",Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=Ndqd7q,
Thailand,THA,9)Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,No,27. Thailand stresses that the right of self-defence must not be used to justify preemptive or preventive use of force in cyberspace.,Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=Ndqd7q,
Thailand,THA,10)Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,No,27. Thailand stresses that the right of self-defence must not be used to justify preemptive or preventive use of force in cyberspace.,Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=Ndqd7q,
Thailand,THA,11)Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"25. Thailand affirms that a malicious cyber operation attributable to a State that causes death, injury, physical damage or destruction equivalent to a traditional non-cyber armed attack, would constitute an armed attack.",Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=Ndqd7q,
Thailand,THA,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,"25. Thailand affirms that a malicious cyber operation attributable to a State that causes death, injury, physical damage or destruction equivalent to a traditional non-cyber armed attack, would constitute an armed attack.",Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=Ndqd7q,
Thailand,THA,12c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,"12d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",Yes,"26. Thailand emphasizes that disruption to essential State functions, absent accompanying physical destruction, injury, or loss of life, does not by itself amount to an armed attack.",Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=Ndqd7q,
Thailand,THA,12e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,No.,The ICJ judgment in Case Concerning Military and Paramilitary Activities in and Against Nicaragua (Nicaragua v. United States of America) indicated that the distinction of the most grave forms of the use of force—those constituting armed attack—and other less forms should be assessed.,Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=Ndqd7q,
Thailand,THA,12f)Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Yes,The ICJ judgment in Case Concerning Military and Paramilitary Activities in and Against Nicaragua (Nicaragua v. United States of America) indicated that the distinction of the most grave forms of the use of force—those constituting armed attack—and other less forms should be assessed.,Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=Ndqd7q,
Thailand,THA,12g)Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,No,"26. Thailand emphasizes that disruption to essential State functions, absent accompanying physical destruction, injury, or loss of life, does not by itself amount to an armed attack.",Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Thailand/Thailand%20(2025).pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=Ndqd7q,
Thailand,THA,"12h)Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,13)Can cyber operations that individually fall below the threshold of an armed attack aggregate into an armed attack triggering the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,14)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation intend to cause harms that qualify the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,15)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation produce reasonably foreseeable harms qualifying the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Ambiguous,"Cyber operations may amount to a use of force if they cause, or are reasonably expected to cause, physical destruction or injury equivalent in gravity to a kinetic attack. For instance, a cyberattack that disables critical infrastructure—such as power grids, air traffic control systems, or hospital networks—resulting in loss of life, injury, or significant material damage, may meet this threshold.",,,
