United Kingdom,GBR,1)Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,"An operation carried out by cyber means may constitute an armed attack giving rise to the inherent right of individual or collective self-defence, as recognised in Article 51 of the UN Charter where the scale and effects of the operation are equivalent to those of an armed attack using kinetic means.",Application of international law to state’s conduct in cyberspace: UK statement,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/United%20Kingdom/United%20Kingdom_2021%20Policy%20Paper_Application%20of%20international%20law%20to%20states’%20conduct%20in%20cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=E5ah65,
United Kingdom,GBR,2)Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Yes,"An operation carried out by cyber means may constitute an armed attack giving rise to the inherent right of individual or collective self-defence, as recognised in Article 51 of the UN Charter where the scale and effects of the operation are equivalent to those of an armed attack using kinetic means.",Application of international law to state’s conduct in cyberspace: UK statement,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/United%20Kingdom/United%20Kingdom_2021%20Policy%20Paper_Application%20of%20international%20law%20to%20states’%20conduct%20in%20cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=E5ah65,
United Kingdom,GBR,2a)Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,3)Does a State’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,4)Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,4a)Can a State  exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,5)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Yes,"The exercise of the inherent right of self-defence against an imminent or on-going armed attack whether by kinetic or cyber means, may itself by by cyber or kinetic means and must always fulfil the requirements of necessity and proportionality.",Application of international law to state’s conduct in cyberspace: UK Statement,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/United%20Kingdom/United%20Kingdom_2021%20Policy%20Paper_Application%20of%20international%20law%20to%20states’%20conduct%20in%20cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=E5ah65,
United Kingdom,GBR,5a)Must force be the only option available to successfully repel and armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,6)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Yes,"The exercise of the inherent right of self-defence against an imminent or on-going armed attack whether by kinetic or cyber means, may itself by by cyber or kinetic means and must always fulfil the requirements of necessity and proportionality.",Application of international law to state’s conduct in cyberspace: UK Statement,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/United%20Kingdom/United%20Kingdom_2021%20Policy%20Paper_Application%20of%20international%20law%20to%20states’%20conduct%20in%20cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=E5ah65,
United Kingdom,GBR,7)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Yes,"The exercise of the inherent right of self-defence against an imminent or on-going armed attack whether by kinetic or cyber means, may itself by by cyber or kinetic means and must always fulfil the requirements of necessity and proportionality.",Application of international law to state’s conduct in cyberspace: UK Statement,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/United%20Kingdom/United%20Kingdom_2021%20Policy%20Paper_Application%20of%20international%20law%20to%20states’%20conduct%20in%20cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=E5ah65,
United Kingdom,GBR,8)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,9)Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,Ambiguous,"Factors in considering the scale and effects of an attack may include the (actual or anticipated) physical destruction of property, injury and death. The exercise of the inherent right of selfdefence against an imminent or on-going armed attack,,,",Application of international law to state’s conduct in cyberspace: UK Statement,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/United%20Kingdom/United%20Kingdom_2021%20Policy%20Paper_Application%20of%20international%20law%20to%20states’%20conduct%20in%20cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=E5ah65,
United Kingdom,GBR,10)Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,11)Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,The circumstances in which the threat or use of force is not unlawful under international law are the same irrespective of whether the conduct is by kinetic or cyber means.,Application of international law to state’s conduct in cyberspace: UK Statement,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/United%20Kingdom/United%20Kingdom_2021%20Policy%20Paper_Application%20of%20international%20law%20to%20states’%20conduct%20in%20cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=E5ah65,
United Kingdom,GBR,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Yes,"Depending on the facts and circumstances in each case, conduct by States carried out in cyberspace is capable of constituting a threat or use of force if the actual or threatened conduct has or would have the same or similar effects of conduct using kinetic means. The circumstances in which the threat or use of force is not unlawful under international law are the same irrespective of whether the conduct is by kinetic or cyber means. ... Whether or not to have recourse to the exercise of the inherent right of self-defence will always be carefully considered having regard to all the circumstances.",Application of international law to state’s conduct in cyberspace: UK Statement,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/United%20Kingdom/United%20Kingdom_2021%20Policy%20Paper_Application%20of%20international%20law%20to%20states’%20conduct%20in%20cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=E5ah65,
United Kingdom,GBR,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,"An operation carried out by cyber means may constitute an armed attack giving rise to the inherent right of individual or collective self-defence, as recognised in Article 51 of the UN Charter where the scale and effects of the operation are equivalent to those of an armed attack using kinetic means. Factors in considering the scale and effects of an attack may include the (actual or anticipated) physical destruction of property, injury and death.",Application of international law to state’s conduct in cyberspace: UK Statement,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/United%20Kingdom/United%20Kingdom_2021%20Policy%20Paper_Application%20of%20international%20law%20to%20states’%20conduct%20in%20cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=E5ah65,
United Kingdom,GBR,12c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Yes,"An operation carried out by cyber means may constitute an armed attack giving rise to the inherent right of individual or collective self-defence, as recognised in Article 51 of the UN Charter where the scale and effects of the operation are equivalent to those of an armed attack using kinetic means. Factors in considering the scale and effects of an attack may include the (actual or anticipated) physical destruction of property, injury and death.",Application of international law to state’s conduct in cyberspace: UK Statement,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/United%20Kingdom/United%20Kingdom_2021%20Policy%20Paper_Application%20of%20international%20law%20to%20states’%20conduct%20in%20cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=E5ah65,
United Kingdom,GBR,"12d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",No,"Factors in considering the scale and effects of an attack may include the (actual or anticipated) physical destruction of property, injury and death.",Application of international law to state’s conduct in cyberspace: UK Statement,https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/United%20Kingdom/United%20Kingdom_2021%20Policy%20Paper_Application%20of%20international%20law%20to%20states’%20conduct%20in%20cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=E5ah65,
