United States,USA,1)Does Article 51 of the UN Charter apply in the cyber context?,Yes,"A State’s inherent right of self-defense, recognized in Article 51 of the UN Charter, may in certain circumstances be triggered by cyber activities that amount to an actual or imminent armed attack.","United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
United States,USA,2)Do States have a right of self-defense in the cyber context as a matter of customary international law independent of the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter?,Yes,"A State’s inherent right of self-defense, recognized in Article 51 of the UN Charter, may in certain circumstances be triggered by cyber activities that amount to an actual or imminent armed attack.","United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
United States,USA,2a)Is the scope of any independent right of self-defense co-extensive with the obligations assumed under Article 51 of the UN Charter,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,3)Does a State’s right of self-defense apply to actions conducted by non-state actors acting without the involvement of another State(s)?,Yes,This inherent right of self-defense against an actual or imminent armed attack in or through cyberspace applies whether the attacker is a State actor or a non-State actor.,"United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
United States,USA,4)Is the right of self-defense triggered solely if an armed attack is attributable to a State according to the applicable rules of customary international law of State responsibility?,No,This inherent right of self-defense against an actual or imminent armed attack in or through cyberspace applies whether the attacker is a State actor or a non-State actor.,"United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
United States,USA,4a)Can a State  exercise self-defense on the basis that the territorial state would be “unwilling and unable” to repress non-state actors whose cyber operations have extraterritorial effects that would rise to the level of an armed attack if conducted by a State?,Silent,"To the extent such cyber operations constitute “attacks” under the law of armed conflict, the rules on conducting attacks must be applied to those cyber operations.","Brian Egan, International Law and Stability in Cyberspace",https://perma.cc/8RU4-AXL2,
United States,USA,5)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of necessity?,Yes,"We have affirmed that a State's inherent right of self-defense, recognized in Article 51 of the U.N. Charter, may in certain circumstances be triggered by cyber activities that amount to an actual or imminent armed attack. The measures taken must be necessary to repel the actual or imminent armed attack. The use of force in self-defense must be limited to what is necessary and proportionate to address the imminent or actual armed attack in or through cyberspace.","DOD General Counsel Remarks at U.S. Cyber Command Leadership Conference United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
United States,USA,5a)Must force be the only option available to successfully repel and armed attack under the right of self-defense?,Ambiguous,"Before resorting to forcible measures in self-defense against an actual or imminent armed attack in or through cyberspace, States should consider whether passive cyber defenses or active defenses below the threshold of the use of force would be sufficient to neutralize the armed attack or imminent threat thereof.","United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
United States,USA,6)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of proportionality?,Yes,The use of force in self-defense must be limited to what is necessary and proportionate to address the imminent or actual armed attack in or through cyberspace.,"United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
United States,USA,7)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of imminence?,Yes,"We have affirmed that a State's inherent right of self-defense, recognized in Article 51 of the U.N. Charter, may in certain circumstances be triggered by cyber activities that amount to an actual or imminent armed attack. The measures taken must be necessary to repel the actual or imminent armed attack.",DOD General Counsel Remarks at U.S Cyber Command Leadership Conference,https://perma.cc/9SBR-6XV9,
United States,USA,8)Is the exercise of the right of self-defense subject to the principle of immediacy?,Yes,"When considering whether an armed attack is imminent under the jus ad bellum for purposes of the initial use of force against a particular non-State actor, the United States analyzes a variety of factors.... These factors include the nature and immediacy of the threat....","Digest of United States Practice in International Law, pgs. 788 (2016).",,
United States,USA,9)Do States have a right of anticipatory or pre-emptive self-defense?,Yes,"We have affirmed that a State's inherent right of self-defense, recognized in Article 51 of the U.N. Charter, may in certain circumstances be triggered by cyber activities that amount to an actual or imminent armed attack.",DOD General Counsel Remarks at U.S Cyber Command Leadership Conference,https://perma.cc/9SBR-6XV9,
United States,USA,10)Do States have a right to preventative self-defense?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,11)Must a cyber operation involve the use of a weapon or weapons to qualify as an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,12)Can a cyber operation alone constitute an armed attack for purposes of triggering the right of self-defense?,Yes,"Although this is necessarily a case-by-case, fact-specific inquiry, cyber activities that proximately result in death, injury, or significant destruction, or represent an imminent threat thereof, would likely be viewed as a use of force / armed attack.","United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
United States,USA,12a)Must a decision that a cyber operation constitute an armed attack triggering a right of self-defense be made on a case-by-case basis or by considering all the circumstances?,Yes,"Although this is necessarily a case-by-case, fact-specific inquiry, cyber activities that proximately result in death, injury, or significant destruction, or represent an imminent threat thereof, would likely be viewed as a use of force / armed attack.","United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
United States,USA,12b) Can a cyber operation qualify as an armed attack under article 51 of the UN Charter if the effects generated are comparable to effects resulting from an action that would otherwise qualify as an armed attack?,Yes,"In determining whether a cyber activity constitutes a use of force prohibited by Article 2(4) of the UN Charter and customary international law or an armed attack sufficient to trigger a State’s inherent right of self-defense, States should consider the nature and extent of injury or death to persons and the destruction of, or damage to, property. Although this is necessarily a case-by-case, fact-specific inquiry, cyber activities that proximately result in death, injury, or significant destruction, or represent an imminent threat thereof, would likely be viewed as a use of force / armed attack. If the physical consequences of a cyber activity result in the kind of damage that dropping a bomb or firing a missile would, that cyber activity should equally be considered a use of force / armed attack.","United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
United States,USA,12c) Must a cyber operation involve consequences of a certain scale and effects to qualify as an attack?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,"12d) Must a cyber operation cause some level of death or injury to persons, or damage or destruction to property, to qualify as an armed attack?",Ambiguous,"If the physical consequences of a cyber activity result in the kind of damage that dropping a bomb or firing a missile would, that cyber activity should equally be considered a use of force / armed attack.","United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
United States,USA,12e) Can any unlawful cyber use of force in violation of Art. 2(4) of the UN Charter trigger a State’s right of self-defense?,Yes,"Cyber activities may in certain circumstances constitute uses of force within the meaning of Article 2(4) of the UN Charter and customary international law. In determining whether a cyber activity constitutes a use of force prohibited by Article 2(4) of the UN Charter and customary international law or an armed attack sufficient to trigger a State’s inherent right of self-defense, States should consider the nature and extent of injury or death to persons and the destruction of, or damage to, property. Although this is necessarily a case-by-case, fact-specific inquiry, cyber activities that proximately result in death, injury, or significant destruction, or represent an imminent threat thereof, would likely be viewed as a use of force / armed attack. If the physical consequences of a cyber activity result in the kind of damage that dropping a bomb or firing a missile would, that cyber activity should equally be considered a use of force / armed attack. ... A State’s inherent right of self-defense, recognized in Article 51 of the UN Charter, may in certain circumstances be triggered by cyber activities that amount to an actual or imminent armed attack.","United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
United States,USA,12f)Do only the most grave uses of force qualify a cyber operation as an armed attack triggering a State’s right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,12g)Can losses of functionality from a cyber operation without accompanying death or injury or damage or destruction to property constitute an armed attack?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,"12h)Can cyber operations producing significant economic effects such as the collapse of a State’s financial system, or the degradation of parts of its economy or critical infrastructure qualify as an armed attack?",Silent,,,,
United States,USA,13)Can cyber operations that individually fall below the threshold of an armed attack aggregate into an armed attack triggering the right of self-defense?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,14)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation intend to cause harms that qualify the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,15)Must the State or entity conducting a cyber operation produce reasonably foreseeable harms qualifying the operation as an armed attack to trigger a right of self-defense in the State that is attacked?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,16)Can cyber operations causing harms sufficient to qualify the operation as an armed attack trigger a third State’s right of self-defense if it was not the target of those operations?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,17)Can non-affected States exercise collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack against an affected State?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,17a) Must a State exercising collective self-defense in response to a cyber operation(s) constituting an armed attack have the victim state request or consent to such actions?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,"18)In its response to a cyber operation or operations qualifying as an armed attack, is the injured state limited to cyber means in response?",No,"There is no requirement that a State defend itself using the same capabilities with which it is being attacked. States may employ cyber capabilities that rise to the level of a use of force as a means of self-defense against a kinetic armed attack (i.e., one that was not launched in or through cyberspace). Additionally, States may in certain circumstances use kinetic military force in selfdefense against an armed attack in or through cyberspace.","United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://tuprd.sharepoint.com/sites/iLITHome/iLIT%20Student%20Researchers/Global%20Cyber%20Law/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/GGE/UN_-Official-compendium-of-national-contributions-on-how-international-law-applies-to-use-of.pdf,
United States,USA,19)Must a State employing the right of self-defense in response to a cyber operation or operations constituting an armed attack report its actions to the UN Security Council?,Silent,,,,
